window.ltssmStateDetails = {
  "Detect.Quiet": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.1.1",
    sourceFile: "detect.quiet.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.1.1\n\nDescription:\n- Transmitter is in an Electrical Idle state. (The DC common mode voltage is not required to be within specification.)\n- 2.5GT/s data rate is selected as the frequency of operation. If the frequency of operation was not 2.5GT/s data rate on entry to this substate, the LTSSM must stay in this substate for at least 1ms, during which the frequency of operation must be changed to the 2.5GT/s data rate.\n\nEntry Actions:\n\nTx Behavior:\n\nRx Expectations:\n\nRegister Updates:\n- **LinkUp** = 0b.\n- The **Equalization 8.0GT/s Phase1, Phase2, Phase3 Successful** and **Equalization 8.0GT/s Complete** bits of the Link Status 2 Register are all set to 0b.\n- The **Equalization 16.0GT/s Phase1, Phase2, Phase3 Successful** and **Equalization 16.0GT/s Complete** bits of the 16GT/s Status Register are all set to 0b.\n- The **Equalization 32.0GT/s Phase1, Phase2, Phase3 Successful** and **Equalization 32.0GT/s Complete** bits of the 32GT/s Status Register are all set to 0b.\n- The **Equalization 64.0GT/s Phase1, Phase2, Phase3 Successful** and **Equalization 64.0GT/s Complete** bits of the 64GT/s Status Register are all set to 0b.\n- The **use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set** variable is reset to 0b.\n- The **Remote L0p Supported** bit in the Device Status 3 Register is reset to 0b.\n- The **Flit_Mode_Enabled** variable is reset to 0b. (When Flit_Mode_Enabled is set to 0b, the link will not operate in Flit Mode.)\n- The **L0p_capable** variable is reset to 0b.\n- The **SRIS_Mode_Enabled** variable is reset to 0b. When the SRIS_Mode_Enabled variable is set to 1b in Flit Mode.\n- The **directed_speed_change** variable is reset to 0b.\n- The **upconfigure_capable** variable is reset to 0b.\n- The **idle_to_rlock_transitioned** variable is reset to 00h.\n- The **select_deemphasis** variable must be set to either 0b or 1b based on platform specific needs for an Upstream Port and identical to the Selectable Preset/De-emphasis bit in the Link Control 2 Register for a Downstream Port.\n- The **equalization_done_8GT_data_rate**, **equalization_done_16GT_data_rate**, **equalization_done_32GT_data_rate**, and **equalization_done_64GT_data_rate** variables are reset to 0b.\n- The **perform_equalization_for_loopback** and **perform_equalization_for_loopback_64GT** variables are reset to 0b.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- Remain at least for **1ms** if data rate must be changed to 2.5 GT/s on entry from a different rate.\n- ** Detect.Active**  After a 12ms timeout or if Electrical Idle Exit is detected on any Lane.\n- May exit earlier if Electrical Idle Exit is detected on any lane.\n\nNotes:\n- If the transition to this state is from **Hot Reset**, it is strongly recommended that the Downstream Port wait for **2ms** before enabling Electrical Idle Exit detection.\n- The SKP Ordered Set transmission frequency must follow the **SRIS mode frequency** for the data rate of operation. Otherwise, the SKP Ordered Set transmission frequency for the SRNS/Common Clock must be followed.\n\n" }
  },
  "Detect.Active": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.1.2",
    sourceFile: "detect.active.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.1.2\n\nDescription:\n- The Transmitter performs a Receiver Detection sequence on all un-configured Lanes that can form one or more Links.\n- The purpose of this substate is to detect whether a far-end Receiver termination is present on the un-configured Lanes.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Initiate Receiver Detection on all un-configured Lanes that can form one or more Links.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- The Transmitter performs the Receiver Detection sequence on all un-configured Lanes that can form one or more Links.\n- If, after a partial Receiver detect result, a second detection attempt is required, the Transmitter again performs the Receiver Detection sequence on all un-configured Lanes that can form one or more Links after waiting **12ms**.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- After the second Receiver Detection sequence, the LTSSM transitions to Polling only if exactly the same Lanes detect a Receiver as in the first Receiver Detection sequence.\n- Otherwise, the LTSSM transitions to Detect.Quiet.\n\nRegister Updates:\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- ** Polling**  If a Receiver is detected on all un-configured Lanes.\n- ** Detect.Quiet**  If a Receiver is not detected on any Lane.\n- If at least one but not all un-configured Lanes detect a Receiver, then:\n  - Wait for **12ms** and perform Receiver Detection again on all un-configured Lanes that can form one or more Links.\n  - ** Polling**  If exactly the same Lanes detect a Receiver as in the first Receiver Detection sequence.\n  - ** Detect.Quiet**  Otherwise.\n\nNotes:\n- If exactly the same Lanes detect a Receiver after the second Receiver Detection sequence, then the Lanes that did not detect a Receiver must:\n  - Be associated with a new LTSSM if this optional feature is supported.\n  - Or, if they cannot be associated with a new LTSSM, transition to Electrical Idle.\n- Lanes that transition to Electrical Idle in this case must be re-associated with the LTSSM immediately after the LTSSM in progress transitions back to Detect.\n- An EIOS does not need to be sent before transitioning to Electrical Idle.\n\n" }
  },
  "Polling.Active": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.2.1",
    sourceFile: "polling.active.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.2.1\n\nDescription:\n- The Transmitter sends TS1 Ordered Sets with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Begin transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n- The Transmitter must drive patterns in the default voltage level of the Transmit Margin field within **192ns** from entry to this state. This transmit voltage level remains in effect until Polling.Compliance or Recovery.RcvrLock is entered.\n- The Transmitter must wait for its TX common mode to settle before exiting Electrical Idle and transmitting the TS1 Ordered Sets.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n- The Data Rate Identifier Symbol of the TS1 Ordered Sets must advertise all data rates between 2.5 GT/s and 32.0 GT/s that the Port supports, including those it does not intend to use.\n  - Note: Ports are not permitted to advertise higher than 32.0 GT/s data rates in this state.\n- A port capable of transmitting at 64.0 GT/s may receive TS1 Ordered Sets with the Flit Mode Supported bit set to 1b and the Supported Link Speeds field set to 1 0111b.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Receive eight consecutive TS1 (or their complement) training sequences on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect, satisfying any of:\n  - TS1 with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD and Compliance_Receive_Request deasserted.\n  - TS1 with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD and Loopback_Request asserted.\n  - TS2 with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD.\n- Any Lane that receives eight consecutive TS1 or TS2 Ordered Sets should have detected an exit from Electrical Idle at least once since entering Polling.Active.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- The **Enter Compliance** bit (bit 4) in the Link Control 2 Register is checked: if set to 1b prior to entry to Polling.Active, transition to Polling.Compliance must be immediate without sending any TS1 Ordered Sets.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- ** Polling.Compliance**  Immediately (without sending any TS1 Ordered Sets) if the Enter Compliance bit (bit 4) in the Link Control 2 Register was set to 1b prior to entry.\n- ** Polling.Configuration**  After at least 1024 TS1 Ordered Sets were transmitted, and all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect receive eight consecutive training sequences (or their complement) satisfying any of:\n  - TS1 with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD and Compliance_Receive_Request deasserted.\n  - TS1 with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD and Loopback_Request asserted.\n  - TS2 with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD.\n- ** Polling.Configuration** (after **24ms** timeout)  If:\n  - (i) Any Lane that detected a Receiver during Detect received eight consecutive training sequences (or their complement) satisfying any of the three conditions above, and a minimum of 1024 TS1 Ordered Sets are transmitted after receiving one TS1 or TS2 Ordered Set, **AND**\n  - (ii) At least a predetermined set of Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect have detected an exit from Electrical Idle at least once since entering Polling.Active.\n  - Note: The predetermined set is implementation specific. Up to [PCIe-1.1] it equaled the total set of Lanes that detected a Receiver.\n- ** Polling.Compliance** (after **24ms** timeout)  Else, if either:\n  - (a) Not all Lanes from the predetermined set in (ii) above have detected an exit from Electrical Idle since entering Polling.Active, **or**\n  - (b) Any Lane that detected a Receiver during Detect received eight consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets (or their complement) with Lane and Link numbers set to PAD, Compliance_Receive_Request asserted, and Loopback_Request deasserted.\n- ** Detect**  Else, if conditions to transition to Polling.Configuration or Polling.Compliance are not met.\n\nNotes:\n- Ports are not permitted to advertise data rates higher than 32.0 GT/s in this state.\n- The transmit voltage level set within 192ns of entry remains in effect until Polling.Compliance or Recovery.RcvrLock is entered.\n- This state may prevent one or more bad Receivers or Transmitters from holding up a valid Link from being configured, and allow for additional training in Polling.Configuration. The exact predetermined set of Lanes is implementation specific.\n- If a passive test load is applied on all Lanes then the device will go to Polling.Compliance.\n\n" }
  },
  "Polling.Configuration": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.2.3",
    sourceFile: "polling.configuration.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.2.3\n\nDescription:\n- Polarity inversion is resolved on entry if required.\n- The substate uses TS2 Ordered Sets (Link and Lane numbers = PAD) to negotiate configuration parameters and advertise supported data rates before transitioning to Configuration.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Receiver must invert polarity if necessary (see  4.2.5.5).\n- The Transmit Margin field of the Link Control 2 Register must be reset to **000b** on entry to this substate.\n- The Transmitter's Polling.Compliance sequence setting is updated, if required, as described in  4.2.7.2.2.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- Transmitter sends TS2 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n- The Data Rate Identifier Symbol of the TS2 Ordered Sets must advertise all data rates between 2.5 GT/s and 32.0 GT/s that the Port supports, including those that it does not intend to use.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD must be received on any Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n- 16 TS2 Ordered Sets must be transmitted after receiving one TS2 Ordered Set before the exit to Configuration is taken.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- **Transmit Margin** field of the Link Control 2 Register  reset to 000b on entry.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- ** Configuration**  If eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets (Link and Lane numbers = PAD) are received on any Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect, and 16 TS2 Ordered Sets are transmitted after receiving one TS2 Ordered Set.\n- ** Detect**  After a **48ms** timeout if the Configuration exit condition is not met.\n\nNotes:\n- Ports are **not** permitted to advertise data rates higher than 32.0 GT/s in this state.\n" }
  },
  "Polling.Compliance": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.2.2",
    sourceFile: "polling.compliance.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.2.2\n\nDescription:\n- This substate is used for compliance pattern generation during device testing. The Transmit Margin field of the Link Control 2 Register is sampled on entry and becomes effective on the transmit package pins within **192ns**; it remains effective for the duration of this substate.\n- The data rate and de-emphasis level for transmitting the compliance pattern are determined on the transition from Polling.Active to Polling.Compliance.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Sample the Transmit Margin field of the Link Control 2 Register; it becomes effective within **192ns** of entry.\n- Determine the compliance pattern data rate and de-emphasis/preset level using the following priority algorithm:\n  1. **If the Port can only transmit at 2.5 GT/s:** Data rate = 2.5 GT/s; de-emphasis = -3.5 dB.\n  2. **Else if entry was triggered by 8 consecutive TS1s in Polling.Active with Compliance_Receive_Request asserted and Loopback_Request deasserted:**\n     - If Port supports 64.0 GT/s and those TS1s had Flit Mode Supported = 1b and Supported Link Speeds = 1 0111b  data rate = 64.0 GT/s.\n     - Else data rate = highest common Data Rate Identifier (Symbol 4 of TS1) across all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n     - `select_deemphasis` = Selectable De-emphasis bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) from those 8 TS1s in Polling.Active.\n     - If common data rate  8.0 GT/s: `select_preset` per Lane = Transmitter Preset from the EQ TS1 Ordered Sets on the corresponding Lane, if not a Reserved encoding (must be used by transmitter; Receiver preset hint is optional at 8.0 GT/s). Lanes that did not receive EQ TS1s with preset info, or received a Reserved encoding, may use any supported Transmitter preset.\n  3. **Else if Enter Compliance bit in Link Control 2 Register is 1b:**\n     - Data rate = Target Link Speed field in Link Control 2 Register.\n     - If data rate = 5.0 GT/s: `select_deemphasis` is set when Compliance Preset/De-emphasis field = 0001b.\n     - If data rate  8.0 GT/s: `select_preset` per Lane = Compliance Preset/De-emphasis Value bits [15:12] in Link Control 2 Register, provided not a Reserved encoding.\n  4. **Else (Enter Compliance not set, no TS1s received):** Data rate, preset, and de-emphasis are taken from Table 4-59 in sequence, cycling through settings based on the number of times Polling.Compliance has been entered with this entry criteria.\n\n### Table 4-59: Compliance Pattern Setting Sequence\n\n| Setting # | Data Rate | Tx De-emphasis or Preset |\n|---|---|---|\n| #1 | 2.5 GT/s | -3.5 dB |\n| #2, #3 | 5.0 GT/s | -3.5 dB, then -6 dB |\n| #4#14 | 8.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0000b1010b (increasing) |\n| #15#25 | 16.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0000b1010b (increasing) |\n| #26#34 | 16.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0100b ( 4.2.4.2) |\n| #35#45 | 32.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0000b1010b (increasing) |\n| #46#54 | 32.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0100b ( 4.2.4.2) |\n| #55#65 | 64.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0000b1010b (increasing) |\n| #66#84 | 64.0 GT/s | Preset Encoding 0000b ( 4.2.4.2) |\n\nSequence repeats on subsequent Polling.Compliance entries. Sequence must be reset to Setting #1 in Polling.Configuration if Port supports  16.0 GT/s or Port Receivers do not meet Z_RX-DC at  8.0 GT/s.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Case A  Entry via TS1s (Compliance_Receive_Request asserted) OR Enter Compliance = 1b AND Enter Modified Compliance = 1b\n- Transmitter sends the **Modified Compliance Pattern** on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect, at the determined data rate, with the error status Symbol initially set to all 0s.\n- If exiting to Polling.Active and data rate  2.5 GT/s or Enter Compliance was 1b: send 8 consecutive EIOSs, enter Electrical Idle; reset data rate to 2.5 GT/s and de-emphasis to -3.5 dB before transitioning.\n\n### Case B  Entry via Enter Compliance = 1b AND Enter Modified Compliance = 0b\n- Transmitter sends the **Compliance Pattern** (standard, not Modified) on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect at the determined data rate and de-emphasis/preset level.\n- On exit: send 8 consecutive EIOSs, enter Electrical Idle; data rate changes to 2.5 GT/s and stabilizes.\n\n### Case C  Entry via Else (Table 4-59 sequence)\n- Transmitter sends the following patterns per setting on Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect:\n  - Settings #1#25, #35#45, #55#65: **Compliance Pattern** on all Lanes.\n  - Setting #26, #46, #66: **Jitter Measurement Pattern** on all Lanes.\n  - Setting #27, #47, #67: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 0/8; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #28, #48, #68: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 1/9; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #29, #49, #69: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 2/10; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #30, #50, #70: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 3/11; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #31, #51, #71: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 4/12; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #32, #52, #72: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 5/13; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #33, #53, #73: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 6/14; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #34, #54, #74: Jitter Measurement on Lanes 7/15; Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #75: **High Swing Toggle Pattern** on all Lanes.\n  - Settings #76#83: High Swing Toggle on Lane pairs (0/8 through 7/15 respectively); Compliance on all others.\n  - Setting #84: **Low Swing Toggle Pattern** on all Lanes.\n- Cycle to next setting on each subsequent Polling.Compliance entry.\n- If compliance pattern data rate  2.5 GT/s and no TS1 Ordered Sets were transmitted in Polling.Active before entering Polling.Compliance: Transmitter must enter Electrical Idle **without** sending EIOSs. Data rate stabilizes in Electrical Idle. De-emphasis/preset is adjusted per `select_deemphasis` or `select_preset`.\n- On exit: If Transmitter was at data rate  2.5 GT/s  send 8 consecutive EIOSs, enter Electrical Idle; data rate changes to 2.5 GT/s and stabilizes.\n- The Transmitter may sequence through Table 4-59 settings and may send one or two consecutive EIOSs prior to entering Electrical Idle between settings (when Flit Mode is supported).\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Modified Compliance Pattern (Case A)\n- **At 2.5/5.0 GT/s:** Each Lane's Receiver independently signals lock by detecting **any one occurrence** of the Modified Compliance Pattern (8b/10b sequence: K28.5, D21.5, K28.5, D10.2, or complements) and sets the **Pattern Lock bit** (bit 7 of the error status Symbol) in its own transmitted Modified Compliance Pattern within **1ms** of receiving the pattern at its Receiver pins.\n  - Error status Symbols must not be used for the lock process (undefined at any given moment).\n- **At  8.0 GT/s:** Error_Status field is initialized to 00h on substate entry. Each Lane independently sets Pattern Lock bit upon achieving **Block Alignment** ( 4.2.2.2.1). After Pattern Lock, each mismatched Symbol in Data Blocks (vs. Idle data Symbol 00h) increments the Receiver Error Count (saturates at 127). Pattern Lock and Receiver Error Count per Lane are transmitted in SKP Ordered Sets of the Modified Compliance Pattern ( 4.2.8). Pattern Lock bit must be set within **4ms** of receiving the Modified Compliance Pattern at Receiver pins.\n- Scrambling requirements ( 4.2.2.4) apply to received Modified Compliance Pattern (LFSR seed set per Lane; EIEOS initializes LFSR; SKP Ordered Sets do not advance LFSR).\n- If data rate = 2.5/5.0 GT/s and a Lane has locked: Receiver Error Count incremented on every Receiver Error; lower 7 bits used as count; saturates at all 1s at 127.\n- No assumptions must be made about received 10-bit patterns in this substate when using 8b/10b encoding.\n\n### Electrical Idle Period (when data rate change required)\n- If Transmitter enters Electrical Idle within Polling.Compliance, the Electrical Idle period must be **> 1ms and  2ms**.\n\nRegister Updates:\n\n| Register | Field / Bits | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Link Control 2 Register | Transmit Margin | Sampled on entry; effective within 192ns; held for duration of substate. |\n| Link Control 2 Register | Target Link Speed | Defines compliance data rate when Enter Compliance = 1b. |\n| Link Control 2 Register | Enter Compliance | Determines entry path; reset to 0b if Upstream Port receives EIOS on any Lane. |\n| Link Control 2 Register | Enter Modified Compliance | Determines if Modified Compliance Pattern is sent (must be 1b with Enter Compliance = 1b). |\n| Link Control 2 Register | Compliance Preset/De-emphasis (bits [3:0]) | If data rate = 5.0 GT/s: `select_deemphasis` is set when this field = 0001b. |\n| Link Control 2 Register | Compliance Preset/De-emphasis Value (bits [15:12]) | If data rate  8.0 GT/s: `select_preset` = this field per Lane, unless Reserved encoding. |\n| TS1 Symbol 4 bit 6 | Selectable De-emphasis | `select_deemphasis` set from the 8 consecutive TS1s received in Polling.Active (TS1-entry path). |\n| TS1 EQ Transmitter Preset (Symbol 4) | Transmitter Preset bits | `select_preset` per Lane set from EQ TS1 Ordered Sets ( 8.0 GT/s), if not Reserved. |\n| Error_Status field | Pattern Lock bit (bit 7) | Set by Receiver on achieving lock to Modified Compliance Pattern. |\n| Error_Status field | Receiver Error Count (bits [6:0]) | Incremented per mismatched Symbol ( 8.0 GT/s); saturates at 127. |\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n| Condition | Next State |\n|---|---|\n| Enter Compliance bit = 0b (not set on entry) | ** Detect** |\n| Enter Compliance = 1b on entry, and now: (a) Enter Compliance bit cleared to 0b, OR (b) Upstream Port receives EIOS on any Lane (Enter Compliance resets to 0b) | ** Polling.Active** (after 8EIOS + Electrical Idle + return to 2.5 GT/s) |\n| Enter Compliance = 1b, Enter Modified Compliance = 0b (Case B): (a) Enter Compliance bit changed to 0b, OR (b) Upstream Port with Enter Compliance = 1b detects EIOS on any Lane | ** Polling.Active** (after 8EIOS + Electrical Idle + return to 2.5 GT/s) |\n| Case C (Table 4-59 sequence): Electrical Idle Exit detected at Receiver of any Lane that detected a Receiver during Detect | ** Polling.Active** (after 8EIOS + Electrical Idle if rate  2.5 GT/s, then return to 2.5 GT/s) |\n| Electrical Idle during Polling.Compliance | Must be > 1ms,  2ms |\n\nNotes:\n- Sending multiple EIOSs provides enough robustness so the other Port detects at least one EIOS and exits Polling.Compliance when the configuration register mechanism was used for entry.\n- If compliance pattern data rate  2.5 GT/s and no TS1 Ordered Sets were transmitted in Polling.Active prior to entry, the Transmitter must enter Electrical Idle **without** transmitting any EIOSs. De-emphasis/preset is adjusted during that Electrical Idle.\n- **Implementation Note (Bit Slip / Block Alignment):** Receivers should ensure they have stabilized before attempting Block Alignment. If bit slips are expected in the first few bits, implementations should wait for that window to pass before settling on Block Alignment. Devices may also want to revalidate Block Alignment prior to setting the Pattern Lock bit.\n- The Table 4-59 sequence must be reset to Setting #1 in Polling.Configuration if the Port supports  16.0 GT/s, or if the Port's Receivers do not meet the Z_RX-DC spec for 2.5 GT/s when operating at  8.0 GT/s.\n- **Implementation Note (Compliance Load Board):** A compliance load board may send a 100 MHz signal for ~1ms on one leg of a differential pair at 350 mV peak-to-peak on any Lane to cycle the device through Table 4-59 settings, starting from the first setting after Fundamental Reset.\n" }
  },
  "Configuration.Linkwidth.Start": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.3.1",
    sourceFile: "configuration_linkwidth_start.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.3.1\n\nDescription:\n- The Downstream Port transmits TS1 Ordered Sets with selected Link numbers and Lane numbers set to PAD to negotiate which Lanes form each Link. The Downstream Port drives Link number selection; transitions to Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept occur upon receiving matching non-PAD Link numbers back from the Upstream Port.\n- The Upstream Port transmits TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD. Upon receiving non-PAD Link numbers from the Downstream Port, it selects a single Link number and responds with that Link number and Lane numbers set to PAD, then transitions to Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- On transition from Polling: any Lane that detected a Receiver during Detect is considered an active Lane.\n- On transition from Recovery: any Lane that was part of the configured Link the previous time through Configuration.Complete is considered an active Lane.\n- On transition from Recovery (if not caused by an LTSSM timeout): the Transmitter must set the **Autonomous Change** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) to 1b in transmitted TS1 Ordered Sets if it intends to change the Link width for autonomous reasons.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Port\n- When `LinkUp` = 0b or the LTSSM is not initiating upconfiguration: transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with selected Link numbers and Lane numbers set to PAD on all active Downstream Lanes.\n- If `upconfigure_capable` = 1b and not initiating upconfiguration: additionally transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with the selected Link number and Lane number set to PAD on each inactive Lane after it detected an exit from Electrical Idle since entering Recovery and has received two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers each set to PAD while in this substate.\n- When `LinkUp` = 1b and initiating upconfiguration of the Link width:\n  - Initially transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with both Link and Lane numbers set to PAD on: the current set of active Lanes; the inactive Lanes intended to be activated; and those Lanes where an exit from Electrical Idle was detected since entering Recovery and two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers each set to PAD have been received.\n  - Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with the selected Link number and Lane number set to PAD when each transmitting Lane receives two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers each set to PAD, or **1 ms** has expired since entry.\n  - After activating any inactive Lane, the Transmitter must wait for its TX common mode to settle before exiting Electrical Idle and transmitting.\n  - Link numbers are only permitted to be different for groups of Lanes capable of being a unique Link.\n- The Data Rate Identifier Symbol of the TS1 Ordered Sets must advertise all data rates that the Port supports, including those not intended to be used.\n- **Crosslink optional** (`LinkUp` = 0b): All Downstream Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect must first transmit 16 to 32 TS1 Ordered Sets with a non-PAD Link number and PAD Lane number. If any Downstream Lanes then receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a non-PAD Link number and Lane number set to PAD, those Lanes are redesignated as Upstream Lanes, a new random T_crosslink timeout is chosen, and the next state is Configuration.Linkwidth.Start as Upstream Lanes.\n\n### Upstream Port\n- Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link numbers and Lane numbers set to PAD on all active Upstream Lanes; the inactive Lanes being initiated to upconfigure; and, if `upconfigure_capable` = 1b, on each inactive Lane where an exit from Electrical Idle was detected since entering Recovery and two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers each set to PAD have been received in this substate.\n- Upon receiving qualifying non-PAD Link numbers (see Rx Expectations): a single Link number is selected and Lane number set to PAD is transmitted on all qualifying Lanes; any leftover Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect continue transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD.\n- The Data Rate Identifier Symbol of the TS1 Ordered Sets must advertise all data rates that the Port supports, including those not intended to be used.\n- **Crosslink optional** (`LinkUp` = 0b): All Upstream Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect must first transmit 16 to 32 TS1 Ordered Sets with PAD Link and Lane numbers. If Upstream Lanes then receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD, the Transmitter continues to send TS1 with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD.\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Downstream Port\n- If any Lanes first received at least one TS1 Ordered Set with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD: the next state is **Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept** immediately after any of those same Downstream Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a non-PAD Link number matching any transmitted Link number and with a Lane number set to PAD.\n  - If crosslink is not supported, the condition of first receiving PAD Link and Lane is always true.\n- Next state is **Disabled** if directed: a Downstream Port instructed by a higher Layer to assert `Disable_Link_Request` (TS1 and TS2) on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect.\n  - Crosslink optional: next state is Disabled after all transmitting-TS1 Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with `Disable_Link_Request` asserted.\n- Next state is **Loopback** if directed by an implementation-specific method and the Transmitter is capable of being a Loopback Lead.\n- Next state is **Loopback** if one of the following conditions is satisfied:\n  - All Lanes transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets that are also receiving TS1 Ordered Sets receive `Loopback_Request` asserted in two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets.\n  - Any Lane transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets receives two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with `Loopback_Request` asserted and Enhanced Link Behavior Control bits set to 01b.\n\n### Upstream Port\n- If any Lane receives two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with non-PAD Link numbers and Lane number set to PAD:\n  - A single Link number is selected and Lane number set to PAD is transmitted on all Lanes that both detected a Receiver and received two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with non-PAD Link numbers and Lane number set to PAD.\n  - Any leftover Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to PAD.\n  - Next state is **Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept**.\n  - If upconfiguring: waits until two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a non-PAD Link Number and PAD Lane number are received on all inactive Lanes to be activated, or **1 ms** after entry, whichever comes first, before transmitting TS1 with the selected Link number.\n- Next state is **Disabled** if directed, or after all transmitting-TS1 Lanes (and also-receiving, for crosslink) receive two consecutive TS1 with `Disable_Link_Request`.\n- Next state is **Loopback** if directed, or upon meeting either Loopback_Request reception condition described above.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- A Port capable of transmitting at 64.0 GT/s may receive TS1 Ordered Sets with the **Flit Mode Supported** bit set to 1b and the **Supported Link Speeds** field set to 1 0111b.\n- **Autonomous Change** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) in transmitted TS1 Ordered Sets: set to 1b if entering this substate from Recovery due to an autonomous Link width change (not an LTSSM timeout).\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream Port\n- **Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept** — After first receiving a TS1 with Link/Lane = PAD, upon receiving two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a non-PAD Link number matching any transmitted Link number and Lane number set to PAD on any qualifying Downstream Lane.\n- **Disabled** — If directed (higher Layer asserts `Disable_Link_Request`), or (crosslink optional) after all transmitting-TS1 Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with `Disable_Link_Request`.\n- **Loopback** — If directed and capable of being Loopback Lead, or upon meeting either Loopback_Request reception condition.\n- **Configuration.Linkwidth.Start** (as Upstream Lanes) — Crosslink optional: after redesignation as Upstream Lanes with new T_crosslink timeout.\n- **Detect** — After a **24 ms** timeout.\n\n### Upstream Port\n- **Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept** — Upon any Lane receiving two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a non-PAD Link number and Lane number set to PAD.\n- **Disabled** — If directed (crosslink only), or after all transmitting-TS1 Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with `Disable_Link_Request`.\n- **Loopback** — If directed, or upon meeting either Loopback_Request reception condition.\n- **Configuration.Linkwidth.Start** (as Downstream Lanes) — Crosslink optional: after T_crosslink timeout (16 to 32 TS2 Ordered Sets with PAD Link and Lane numbers are sent; Upstream Lanes become Downstream Lanes).\n- **Detect** — After a **24 ms** timeout.\n\nNotes:\n- **Link number assignment example**: A set of eight Downstream Lanes capable of negotiating to become one x8 Port or two x4 Ports sends TS1 Ordered Sets with Link number N on four Lanes and Link number N+1 on the other four Lanes. Lane numbers are all set to PAD.\n- The device receiving an Ordered Set with `Loopback_Request` asserted becomes the Loopback Follower. If the Loopback Follower supports 1b/1b encoding, it must take into account whether SRIS clocking is enabled (Symbol 4, Bit 7) for identifying SKP OS boundaries during Loopback.\n- Crosslink: Receiving a sequence of TS1 Ordered Sets with a PAD Link number followed by a non-PAD Link number matching the transmitted Link number is only valid when not interrupted by a TS2 Ordered Set.\n- **Crosslink deadlock resolution**: both Ports become Upstream and assign a random T_crosslink timeout until one side takes the role of Downstream Port. The timeout must be random even when connecting two identical devices.\n- **Recommendation for multi-Lane Link error recovery**: delay evaluation of received Link numbers by an additional two or more TS1 Ordered Sets (8b/10b) or 34 or more TS1 Ordered Sets (128b/130b or 1b/1b encoding), but must not exceed **1 ms**, to avoid prematurely configuring a smaller Link than possible.\n- After activating any inactive Lane, the Transmitter must wait for its TX common mode to settle before exiting Electrical Idle and transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets.\n" }
  },
  "Configuration.Linkwidth.Accept": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.3.2",
    sourceFile: "configuration.linkwidth.accept.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.3.2\n\nDescription:\n- This sub-state handles Link width acceptance for both Downstream and Upstream Lanes after the Downstream Port has transmitted TS1 Ordered Sets with assigned Link and Lane numbers.\n- On success, the next state is **Configuration.Lanenum.Wait**.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Downstream Port: transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with the assigned Link number and unique non-PAD Lane numbers on all Lanes in the configured group.\n- Upstream Port: transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with the same non-PAD Link number and Lane numbers that, if possible, match the received Lane numbers, or different Lane numbers if needed (for example, when Lane reversal is used).\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with the same Link number and unique non-PAD Lane numbers assigned to all Lanes in the configured group.\n- The assigned non-PAD Lane numbers must range from **0 to n-1** and be assigned sequentially to the same grouping of Lanes receiving the same Link number.\n- Downstream Lanes not receiving TS1 Ordered Sets must not disrupt the initial sequential numbering of the widest possible Link.\n- Any left-over Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with the same non-PAD Link number and Lane numbers that, if possible, match the received Lane numbers or differ if necessary (for example, Lane reversal).\n- The newly assigned Lane numbers must range from **0 to m-1**, be assigned sequentially only to a continuous grouping of Lanes receiving non-PAD Lane numbers, include either Lane 0 or Lane n-1 (largest received Lane number), and use **m-1** as the largest received Lane number (**n-1**).\n- Remaining Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n- The received consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets may be either standard TS1 Ordered Sets or Modified TS1 Ordered Sets. Modified TS1 Ordered Sets are only received when the conditions to set `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` are met.\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- A configured Link can be formed with at least one group of Lanes that received two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with:\n  - the same received non-PAD Link number, and\n  - a Link number that matches one transmitted by the Downstream Lanes.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- A configured Link can be formed using Lanes that transmitted a non-PAD Link number and are receiving two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with:\n  - the same non-PAD Link number, and\n  - any non-PAD Lane number.\n\nRegister Updates:\n\n### `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set`\n- Set to 1b if **all** of the following are true:\n  - `LinkUp` = 0b.\n  - The component transmitted the **Modified TS1/TS2 Ordered Sets Supported** value (`11b`) in the Enhanced Link Behavior Control field (Symbol 5) of all TS1 and TS2 Ordered Sets in Polling and Configuration since entering Polling.\n  - The received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets on all Lanes of the configured Link that caused the transition from Polling.Configuration to Configuration had the **Modified TS1/TS2 Ordered Sets Supported** value (`11b`) in the Enhanced Link Behavior Control field (Symbol 5), and the 32.0 GT/s data rate supported bit was set to 1b.\n\n### `Flit_Mode_Enabled`\n- Set to 1b if **all** of the following are true:\n  - `LinkUp` = 0b.\n  - The component transmitted the **Flit Mode Supported** bit in the Data Rate Identifier field (Symbol 4 bit 0) in TS1 and TS2 Ordered Sets in Polling and Configuration since entering Polling.\n  - The received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets on all Lanes of the currently configured Link that caused the transition from Polling.Configuration to Configuration had the **Flit Mode Supported** bit set to 1b in the Data Rate Identifier field.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **Configuration.Lanenum.Wait** — If a configured Link can be formed:\n  - Downstream: at least one group of Lanes received two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a matching non-PAD Link number.\n  - Upstream: Lanes transmitted a non-PAD Link number and are receiving two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with the same non-PAD Link number and any non-PAD Lane number.\n- **Detect** — After a **2 ms** timeout, or if no Link can be configured, or if all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\nNotes:\n- If a possible multi-Lane Link receives an error in a TS1 Ordered Set, loses 128b/130b Block Alignment, or loses Block/Flit alignment with 1b/1b encoding on a subset of received Lanes, it is recommended to delay evaluation by:\n  - an additional **two or more** TS1 Ordered Sets when using 8b/10b encoding, or\n  - an additional **34 or more** TS1 Ordered Sets when using 128b/130b or 1b/1b encoding.\n- That delay must not exceed **1 ms**, to avoid prematurely configuring a smaller Link than possible.\n- **Negotiating Flit Mode and Modified TS1/TS2 usage is orthogonal** — a device can advertise and negotiate support for neither, either, or both. This applies to both Downstream and Upstream Ports.\n\n### Implementation Note: Example Cases\n\n**Downstream Lanes**\n1. A x8 Downstream Port divisible into two x4 Links sends two different Link numbers to two x4 Upstream Ports. The Upstream Ports may respond simultaneously by picking the two Link numbers. The Downstream Port then chooses one set of Link numbers to configure as a Link and leaves the other for a secondary LTSSM to configure.\n2. A x8 Downstream Port where only seven Lanes are receiving TS1 Ordered Sets with the same received non-PAD Link number, while an eighth Lane is not receiving a TS1 Ordered Set, still treats the eighth Lane consistently with the other seven for widest-Link numbering.\n\n**Upstream Lanes**\n1. A x8 Upstream Port presented with Lane numbers opposite from its preferred numbering:\n   - If optional Lane reversal is supported, the Upstream Port transmits the same Lane numbers back.\n   - Otherwise, the opposite Lane numbers are sent back, and the Downstream Port must optionally fix the ordering or exit Configuration.\n2. A x8 Upstream Port not receiving TS1 Ordered Sets on Upstream Port Lane 0:\n   - If the Upstream Port supports only x8 or x1 Link **and** supports Lane reversal: assign Lane 0 only to the received Lane 7 (received Lane number **n-1**); the remaining seven Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n   - If the Upstream Port supports only x8 or x1 Link **and** does not support Lane reversal: no Link can be formed; the Upstream Port eventually times out after **2 ms** and exits to Detect.\n3. An optional x8 Upstream crosslink Port divisible into two x4 Links attached to two x4 Downstream Ports presenting the same Link number and Lane numbers 0 to 3 simultaneously chooses one set of Lane numbers to configure as a Link and leaves the other for a second pass through Configuration.\n" }
  },
  "Configuration.Lanenum.Accept": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.3.3",
    sourceFile: "configuration.lanenum.accept.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.3.3\n\nDescription:\n- In this sub-state, if the `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` variable is set to 1b:\n  - Transmitter must send Modified TS1 Ordered Sets instead of TS1 Ordered Sets.\n  - Receiver must check for receipt of Modified TS1 Ordered Sets instead of TS1 Ordered Sets. [Note: See  Section 4.2.5.1 for the definition of identical consecutive modified TS1 Ordered Sets.]\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Apply Modified TS1 Ordered Set rules if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b: Transmit Modified TS1 Ordered Sets (Downstream and Upstream) and expect Modified TS1 Ordered Sets on receive.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Continue transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets (or Modified TS1 Ordered Sets if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b) with the previously assigned non-PAD Link and Lane numbers on configured Lanes.\n- If a configured Link cannot be formed with the current Lane assignment, transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with new Lane numbers (0 to m-1) on any subset of Lanes receiving non-PAD Lane numbers, as described under Timing/Exit Conditions.\n- Any left-over Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Continue transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets (or Modified TS1 Ordered Sets if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b) with the previously assigned non-PAD Link and Lane numbers on configured Lanes.\n- If a configured Link cannot be formed, transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with new Lane numbers (0 to m-1) on any subset of Lanes receiving non-PAD Lane numbers.\n- Any left-over Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets (or Modified TS1 Ordered Sets if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b) must be received with non-PAD Link and non-PAD Lane numbers that match all the non-PAD Link and non-PAD Lane numbers (or reversed Lane numbers if Lane reversal is optionally supported) being transmitted in Downstream Lane TS1 Ordered Sets. The next state is **Configuration.Complete**.\n  - The condition of Reversed Lane numbers is defined strictly as: Downstream Lane 0 receiving a TS1 Ordered Set with a Lane number equal to n-1 **and** Downstream Lane n-1 receiving a TS1 Ordered Set with a Lane number equal to 0.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Two consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets must be received with non-PAD Link and non-PAD Lane numbers that match all non-PAD Link and non-PAD Lane numbers being transmitted in Upstream Lane TS1 Ordered Sets. The next state is **Configuration.Complete**.\n  - If `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` is Set, an Alternate Protocol Negotiation was performed, and the Downstream Port decided not to use any Alternate Protocol, the received TS2 Ordered Sets will have Modified TS Usage set to a value as defined in  Table 4-39. See  Section 4.2.5.2 for Alternate Protocol Negotiation details.\n\nRegister Updates:\n\n### `SRIS_Mode_Enabled`\n- Set to 1b if **all** of the following are true:\n\n**Downstream Lanes:**\n- `LinkUp` = 0b\n- The Port has been transmitting SRIS Clocking (Symbol 4, bit 7 = 1b) in the transmitted TS1 Ordered Sets since it entered Configuration state.\n\n**Upstream Lanes:**\n- `LinkUp` = 0b\n- Any configured Lane in the Port has the SRIS Clocking bit set (Symbol 4, bit 7 = 1b) in the two consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets.\n\n### Link Bandwidth Management / Link Autonomous Bandwidth Status (Downstream only)\n- Update on a Link bandwidth change if the current transition to Configuration came from Recovery:\n  - If the bandwidth change was initiated by the Downstream Port due to reliability issues, set the **Link Bandwidth Management Status** bit.\n  - Else if the bandwidth change was not initiated by the Downstream Port and the **Autonomous Change** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) in two consecutive received TS1 Ordered Sets is 0b, set the **Link Bandwidth Management Status** bit.\n  - Else, set the **Link Autonomous Bandwidth Status** bit.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- **Configuration.Complete** — If two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets are received with non-PAD Link and non-PAD Lane numbers matching all non-PAD Link/Lane numbers being transmitted (or reversed Lane numbers if Lane reversal is optionally supported).\n  - If `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` is Set and an Alternate Protocol Negotiation is being performed, the transition to Configuration.Complete must be delayed for **10 s** or until the Downstream Port receives the Upstream Port's response to that protocol request (whichever happens first). See  Section 4.2.5.2.\n  - Note: Retimers are permitted to delay the transition to Configuration.Complete, as described in  Section 4.3.9.\n- **Configuration.Lanenum.Wait** — If a configured Link can be formed with any subset of the Lanes that receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with the same transmitted non-PAD Link numbers and any non-PAD Lane numbers. TS1 Ordered Sets are transmitted with the same non-PAD Link numbers and newly assigned Lane numbers.\n  - The newly assigned Lane numbers must range from **0 to m-1**, be assigned sequentially only to a continuous grouping of Lanes receiving non-PAD Lane numbers (Lanes not receiving any TS1 Ordered Sets always disrupt a continuous grouping and must not be included), include either Lane 0 or Lane n-1 (largest received Lane number), and use **m-1** as the largest received Lane number (**n-1**). Any left-over Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n- **Detect** — If no Link can be configured or if all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- **Configuration.Complete** — If two consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets are received with non-PAD Link and non-PAD Lane numbers matching all non-PAD Link/Lane numbers being transmitted in Upstream Lane TS1 Ordered Sets.\n  - Note: Retimers are permitted to delay the transition to Configuration.Complete, as described in  Section 4.3.9.\n- **Configuration.Lanenum.Wait** — If a configured Link can be formed with any subset of the Lanes that receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with the same transmitted non-PAD Link numbers and any non-PAD Lane numbers. TS1 Ordered Sets are transmitted with the same non-PAD Link numbers and newly assigned Lane numbers.\n  - The newly assigned Lane numbers must range from **0 to m-1**, be assigned sequentially only to a continuous grouping of Lanes receiving non-PAD Lane numbers, include either Lane 0 or Lane n-1 (largest received Lane number), and use **m-1** as the largest received Lane number (**n-1**). Any left-over Lanes must transmit TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n- **Detect** — If no Link can be configured or if all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\nNotes:\n- If any possible multi-Lane Link received an error in a TS1 Ordered Set, lost 128b/130b Block Alignment, or lost Block/Flit alignment with 1b/1b encoding on a subset of the received Lanes, it is recommended to delay evaluation to avoid configuring a smaller Link than is possible:\n  - When using **8b/10b encoding**: delay by an additional two or more TS1 Ordered Sets, but must not delay by more than **1 ms**.\n  - When using **128b/130b or 1b/1b encoding**: delay by an additional 34 or more TS1 Ordered Sets, but must not delay by more than **1 ms**.\n\n### Implementation Note: Clocking with SRIS Mode vs. Non-SRIS Mode\nClocking with SRIS mode vs. non-SRIS mode advertisement was added for Flit Mode support since there is no sync hdr in the 1b/1b encoding. It is advertised by the Downstream Port. However, it is defined as orthogonal to Flit Mode support. This note applies to the Downstream and Upstream Ports.\n" }
  },
  "Configuration.Lanenum.Wait": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.3.4",
    sourceFile: "configuration.lanenum.wait.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.3.4\n\nDescription:\n- In this sub-state, if the `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` variable is set to 1b:\n  - Transmitter must send Modified TS1 Ordered Sets instead of TS1 Ordered Sets.\n  - Receiver must check for receipt of Modified TS1 Ordered Sets instead of TS1 Ordered Sets, though it may receive TS1 Ordered Sets initially while the Link partner is transitioning to this sub-state. [Note: These must be identical consecutive Modified TS1 Ordered Sets with valid parity in the last Symbol.]\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Apply Modified TS1 Ordered Set rules if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b: Transmit Modified TS1 Ordered Sets and expect Modified TS1 Ordered Sets on receive (allowing initial TS1 Ordered Sets from a Link partner still transitioning).\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets (or Modified TS1 Ordered Sets if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b) with assigned Link and Lane numbers.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Transmit TS1 Ordered Sets (or Modified TS1 Ordered Sets if `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b) with assigned Link and Lane numbers.\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Monitor all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect for two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a Lane number different from when the Lane first entered Configuration.Lanenum.Wait, and for TS1 Ordered Sets on all Lanes matching what is being transmitted.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Monitor all Lanes for two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a Lane number different from when the Lane first entered Configuration.Lanenum.Wait, and for any Lane receiving two consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets.\n\nRegister Updates:\n*(No register updates specified for this sub-state.)*\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- **Configuration.Lanenum.Accept** — If any of the Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with a Lane number different from when the Lane first entered Configuration.Lanenum.Wait, and not all the Lanes' Link numbers are set to PAD; **or** two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets have been received on all Lanes with Link and Lane numbers that match what is being transmitted on all Lanes.\n  - Upstream Lanes are permitted to delay up to **1 ms** before transitioning to Configuration.Lanenum.Accept to prevent received errors or skew between Lanes affecting the final configured Link width.\n  - The condition of requiring reception of any Lane number different from when the Lane(s) first entered Configuration.Lanenum.Wait is necessary to allow the two Ports to settle on an agreed-upon Link width. A Lane number must have changed from when the Lanes most recently entered Configuration.Lanenum.Wait before a transition to Configuration.Lanenum.Accept can occur.\n- **Detect** — After a **2 ms** timeout, or if all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- **Configuration.Lanenum.Accept** — If either of the following is true:\n  - a. Any of the Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets that have a Lane number different from when the Lane first entered Configuration.Lanenum.Wait, and not all the Lanes' Link numbers are set to PAD; **or**\n  - b. Any Lane receives two consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets.\n- **Detect** — After a **2 ms** timeout, or if all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers set to **PAD**.\n\nNotes:\n*(No additional notes specified for this sub-state.)*\n" }
  },
  "Configuration.Complete": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.3.5",
    sourceFile: "configuration.complete.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.3.5\n\nDescription:\n- A Port is allowed to change the supported data rates it advertises when it enters this substate, but must not change those values while in this substate.\n- If `Flit_Mode_Enabled` = 0b and `LinkUp` = 1b, a Port is permitted to change the **Upconfigure** bit it advertises when it enters this substate, but must not change it while in this substate.\n- If `Flit_Mode_Enabled` = 1b and `LinkUp` = 0b, a Port is permitted to change the **L0p Capability** it advertises when it enters this substate, but must not change it while in this substate.\n- If `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b: Transmitter must send Modified TS2 Ordered Sets instead of TS2 Ordered Sets, and Receiver must check for receipt of Modified TS2 Ordered Sets instead of TS2 Ordered Sets.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- N/A\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Transmit TS2 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers matching the received TS1 Ordered Set Link and Lane numbers.\n- The **Link Upconfigure / L0p Capability** bit of the TS2 Ordered Sets may be set to 1b to indicate capability to support down to a x1 Link on Lane 0 and up-configure the Link while `LinkUp` = 1b. Advertising this capability is optional.\n- `N_FTS` must be noted for use in L0s when leaving this state.\n- When using 8b/10b encoding, Lane-to-Lane de-skew must be completed when leaving this state.\n- Scrambling is disabled if all configured Lanes have the **Disable Scrambling** bit asserted in two consecutively received TS2 Ordered Sets. The Port sending the Disable Scrambling bit must also disable scrambling. Scrambling can only be disabled when using 8b/10b encoding.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Transmit TS2 Ordered Sets with Link and Lane numbers matching the received TS2 Link and Lane numbers.\n- The **Link Upconfigure / L0p Capability** bit of the TS2 Ordered Sets may be set to 1b to indicate capability to support a x1 Link on Lane 0 and up-configure the Link while `LinkUp` = 1b. Advertising this capability is optional.\n- `N_FTS` must be noted for use in L0s when leaving this state.\n- When using 8b/10b encoding, Lane-to-Lane de-skew must be completed when leaving this state.\n- Scrambling is disabled if all configured Lanes have the **Disable Scrambling** bit asserted in two consecutively received TS2 Ordered Sets. The Port sending the Disable Scrambling bit must also disable scrambling. Scrambling can only be disabled when using 8b/10b encoding.\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Next state is **Configuration.Idle** immediately after all transmitting Lanes receive eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with matching non-PAD Link and Lane numbers and identical data rate identifiers (including identical **Link Upconfigure / L0p Capability** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6)), and 16 TS2 Ordered Sets are sent after receiving one TS2 Ordered Set.\n- If the **Retimer Presence Detect Supported** bit (Link Capabilities 2 Register) = 1b: must also receive identical **Retimer Present** (Symbol 5 bit 4) at 2.5 GT/s.\n- If the **Two Retimers Presence Detect Supported** bit = 1b: must also receive identical **Retimer Present** (Symbol 5 bits 5:4) at 2.5 GT/s.\n- At 2.5 GT/s, update **Retimer Presence Detected** and **Two Retimers Presence Detected** bits in the Link Status 2 Register based on the received TS2 Ordered Sets.\n- If the device supports > 2.5 GT/s: record the data rate identifier received on any configured Lane; `changed_speed_recovery` is reset to 0b.\n- If `Flit_Mode_Enabled` = 0b: if both transmitted and received TS2 have the **Link Upconfigure / L0p Capability** bit = 1b, `upconfigure_capable` is set to 1b; else it is reset to 0b.\n- If `Flit_Mode_Enabled` = 1b and `LinkUp` = 0b: if both transmitted and received TS2 have the **Link Upconfigure / L0p Capability** bit = 1b, `L0p_capable` is set to 1b and the **Remote L0p Supported** bit in the Device Status 3 Register is set to 1b.\n- All remaining Lanes not part of the configured Link are no longer associated with the LTSSM in progress and must either:\n  1. Be associated with a new LTSSM if this optional feature is supported.\n  2. Transition to Electrical Idle. Lanes that formed a Link up to L0 with `LinkUp` = 1b but are not part of the current configured Link must be associated with the same LTSSM if it advertises Link width upconfigure capability. It is recommended that Receiver terminations be left on; if not, they must be turned on when the LTSSM enters Recovery.RcvrCfg through Configuration.Complete if `upconfigure_capable` = 1b. Any Lane not part of the LTSSM during initial Link training through L0 cannot join via Link width upconfiguration. These Lanes must be re-associated with the LTSSM immediately after it transitions back to Detect. An EIOS does not need to be sent before transitioning to Electrical Idle.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Next state is **Configuration.Idle** immediately after all transmitting Lanes receive eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with matching non-PAD Link and Lane numbers and identical data rate identifiers (including identical **Link Upconfigure / L0p Capability** bit), and 16 consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets are sent after receiving one TS2 Ordered Set.\n- Same Retimer Presence Detect register update rules as Downstream Lanes (above).\n- Same `upconfigure_capable` / `L0p_capable` / `changed_speed_recovery` update rules as Downstream Lanes (above).\n- Remaining Lanes not part of the configured Link must either:\n  1. Optionally be associated with a new crosslink LTSSM.\n  2. Transition to Electrical Idle. Receiver terminations are required to meet Z_RX-HIGH-IMP-DC-POS and Z_RX-HIGH-IMP-DC-NEG. These Lanes must be re-associated with the LTSSM immediately after it transitions back to Detect. EIOS does not need to be sent before transitioning to Electrical Idle.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- If `Flit_Mode_Enabled` = 0b and `upconfigure_capable` = 1b: `upconfigure_capable` variable is updated.\n- If `Flit_Mode_Enabled` = 1b and `LinkUp` = 0b and L0p capable: `L0p_capable` set to 1b; **Remote L0p Supported** bit in Device Status 3 Register set to 1b.\n- **Retimer Presence Detected** and **Two Retimers Presence Detected** bits in Link Status 2 Register updated if applicable (2.5 GT/s data rate).\n- `changed_speed_recovery` reset to 0b when device supports > 2.5 GT/s.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **Configuration.Idle** — After all transmitting Lanes receive eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with matching non-PAD Link/Lane numbers, identical data rate identifiers, and 16 TS2 Ordered Sets sent after receiving one TS2 Ordered Set.\n- After a **2 ms** timeout:\n  - **Detect** — if the current data rate is 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s.\n  - **Configuration.Idle** — if `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` < FFh and current data rate is 8.0 GT/s or higher. In this case: `changed_speed_recovery` is reset to 0b; Lanes not part of the configured Link are no longer associated with the LTSSM; `upconfigure_capable` may be optionally updated if at least one Lane received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with matching Lane and Link numbers.\n  - **Detect** — otherwise.\n\nNotes:\n- A Port may update its advertised supported data rates upon entering this substate but must not change them while in this substate.\n- `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` = 1b causes Modified TS2 Ordered Sets to be used in place of standard TS2 Ordered Sets for both Tx and Rx.\n- Scrambling is only disabled via 8b/10b encoding when the **Disable Scrambling** bit is asserted in two consecutive received TS2 Ordered Sets across all configured Lanes.\n- Lanes exiting the configured Link that cannot join a new LTSSM must transition to Electrical Idle and are re-associated with the LTSSM immediately after it transitions back to Detect.\n- For optional crosslinks (Downstream), Receiver terminations on unused Lanes must meet Z_RX-HIGH-IMP-DC-POS and Z_RX-HIGH-IMP-DC-NEG.\n- An EIOS does not need to be sent before transitioning to Electrical Idle on unused Lanes, and the transition does not need to occur on a Symbol or Ordered Set boundary.\n" }
  },
  "Configuration.Idle": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.3.6",
    sourceFile: "configuration.idle.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.3.6\n\nDescription:\n- When using 8b/10b encoding: Transmitter sends Idle data Symbols on all configured Lanes in Non-Flit Mode, and IDLE Flits across all configured Lanes in Flit Mode.\n- When using 128b/130b encoding in Non-Flit Mode:\n  - At 8.0 GT/s: Transmitter sends one SDS Ordered Set on all configured Lanes to start a Data Stream and then sends Idle data Symbols. The first Idle data Symbol on Lane 0 is the first Symbol of the Data Stream.\n  - At 16.0 GT/s or higher: Transmitter sends one Control SKP Ordered Set followed immediately by one SDS Ordered Set on all configured Lanes to start a Data Stream and then sends Idle data Symbols.\n- When using 1b/1b encoding or 128b/130b encoding in Flit Mode: Transmitter sends an SDS Ordered Set sequence followed by one Control SKP Ordered Set on all configured Lanes to start a Data Stream with IDLE Flits.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- `LinkUp` is set to 1b.\n- If `LinkUp` = 0b and 64.0 GT/s data rate is supported by all components in the Link:\n  - If the **No Equalization Needed** bit (Symbol 5 bit 1) was set to 1b in both the received and transmitted Modified TS2 Ordered Sets (or Enhanced Link Behavior Control = 10b in both received and transmitted TS2 Ordered Sets): `equalization_done_8GT`, `equalization_done_16GT`, `equalization_done_32GT`, and `equalization_done_64GT` variables are each set to 1b. The **No Equalization Needed** bit in the 64.0 GT/s Status Register is set to 1b.\n  - Else if the **Equalization Bypass to Highest NRZ Rate** bit (Symbol 5 bit 0) was set to 1b in both received and transmitted Modified TS2 Ordered Sets (or Enhanced Link Behavior Control = 01b or 10b in both): `equalization_done_8GT` and `equalization_done_16GT` variables are each set to 1b.\n  - If entry was caused by eight consecutive identical Modified TS2 Ordered Sets and `LinkUp` = 0b, and the **Modified TS Usage** field = 010b (Alternate Protocols) in both transmitted and received Modified TS2 Ordered Sets with matching Modified TS Information 1 and Alternate Protocol Vendor ID fields: the **Modified TS Received** bit in the 32.0 GT/s Status Register is set to 1b.\n- If `LinkUp` = 0b and 32.0 GT/s data rate is supported by all components in the Link:\n  - If **No Equalization Needed** (Symbol 5 bit 1) = 1b in both received and transmitted Modified TS2 (or Enhanced Link Behavior Control = 10b in both TS2): `equalization_done_8GT`, `equalization_done_16GT`, and `equalization_done_32GT` are set to 1b. **No Equalization Needed Received** bit in 32.0 GT/s Status Register is set to 1b.\n  - Else if **Equalization Bypass to Highest NRZ Rate** (Symbol 5 bit 0) = 1b in both received and transmitted Modified TS2 (or Enhanced Link Behavior Control = 01b or 10b in both TS2): `equalization_done_8GT` and `equalization_done_16GT` are set to 1b.\n  - If entry was caused by eight consecutive identical Modified TS2 Ordered Sets and `LinkUp` = 0b, with matching Modified TS Usage, Modified TS Information 1, and Alternate Protocol Vendor ID fields: **Modified TS Received** bit in the 32.0 GT/s Status Register is set to 1b.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- When using 8b/10b encoding: Transmitter sends Idle data Symbols on all configured Lanes in Non-Flit Mode; IDLE Flits in Flit Mode.\n- When using 128b/130b encoding in Non-Flit Mode at 8.0 GT/s: send one SDS Ordered Set then Idle data Symbols. The first Idle data Symbol on Lane 0 is the first Symbol of the Data Stream.\n- When using 128b/130b encoding in Non-Flit Mode at 16.0 GT/s or higher: send one Control SKP Ordered Set followed immediately by one SDS Ordered Set then Idle data Symbols.\n- When using 1b/1b encoding or 128b/130b encoding in Flit Mode: send an SDS Ordered Set sequence followed by one Control SKP Ordered Set then IDLE Flits.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Receiver waits for Idle data in Non-Flit Mode and for IDLE Flits in Flit Mode.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- `LinkUp` = 1b (set on entry).\n- Equalization-related variable and register updates are performed on entry based on negotiated data rate and Equalization Bypass conditions (see Entry Actions above).\n- On transition to **L0**:\n  - `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` is reset to 0b.\n  - `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` is reset to 00h.\n  - If software has written 1b to the **Retrain Link** bit in the Link Control Register since the last transition to L0 from Recovery or Configuration, the Downstream Port must set the **Link Bandwidth Management Status** bit of the Link Status Register to 1b.\n- On transition to **Recovery.RcvrLock**:\n  - At 8.0 GT/s or higher: `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` is incremented by 1.\n  - At 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s: `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` is set to FFh and `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` is reset to 0b.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **L0** (8b/10b, Non-Flit Mode) — Eight consecutive Symbol Times of Idle data received on all configured Lanes and 16 Idle data Symbols sent after receiving one Idle data Symbol.\n- **L0** (128b/130b, Non-Flit Mode) — Eight consecutive Symbol Times of Idle data received on all configured Lanes and one Idle data Symbol sent. Lane-to-Lane de-skew must be completed before Data Stream processing starts.\n- **L0** (Flit Mode) — Two consecutive IDLE Flits received and the minimum number of IDLE Flits sent after receiving one IDLE Flit (minimum: 4 for 8b/10b or 128b/130b; 8 for 1b/1b), provided this state was not entered by a timeout from Configuration.Complete. Lane-to-Lane de-skew must be completed.\n- **Recovery.RcvrLock** — After a minimum **2 ms** timeout, if `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` < FFh.\n- **Detect** — After a minimum **2 ms** timeout, if `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` = FFh.\n\nNotes:\n- If software has written 1b to the **Retrain Link** bit since the last transition to L0 from Recovery or Configuration, the Downstream Port must set the **Link Bandwidth Management Status** bit in the Link Status Register to 1b (applies to both 8b/10b Non-Flit and Flit Mode L0 transitions).\n- The `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` and `use_modified_TS1_TS2_Ordered_Set` variable resets on L0 transition are encoding/mode dependent (see Register Updates).\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.RcvrLock": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.1",
    sourceFile: "recovery.rcvrlock.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.1\n\nDescription:\n- If the Link is operating at 8.0 GT/s or higher, a Receiver must consider any TS0, TS1, or TS2 Ordered Set to be received only after it obtains Block Alignment in that Lane.\n- If entry to this substate is from L1, Recovery.Speed, or L0s: Block Alignment must be obtained after exiting Electrical Idle condition.\n- If entry to this substate is from L0: Block Alignment must be obtained after the end of the last Data Stream.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- The **Transmit Margin** field of the Link Control 2 Register is sampled on entry and becomes effective on the transmit package pins within **192 ns** of entry; it remains effective until a new value is sampled on a subsequent entry from L0, L0s, or L1.\n- If a **Downstream Port** intends to use the Upstream Port's de-emphasis information in Recovery.RcvrCfg, it must record the value of the **Selectable De-emphasis** bit received in this state.\n- `successful_speed_negotiation` is reset to 0b upon entry.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- Transmitter sends TS1 Ordered Sets on all configured Lanes using the same Link and Lane numbers set after leaving Configuration.\n- The `speed_change` bit (bit 7 of Data Rate Identifier Symbol in TS1) must be set to 1b if `directed_speed_change` = 1b. `directed_speed_change` is set to 1b if any configured Lane receives eight consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with `speed_change` = 1b.\n- Only data rates > 2.5 GT/s that can be supported reliably should be advertised. In Non-Flit Mode, `N_FTS` in the TS1 Ordered Set reflects the number at the current speed. A device may change the supported data rates it advertises when entering this substate.\n- A **Downstream Port** intending to redo equalization from 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s to 8.0 GT/s or 32.0 GT/s (when Equalization Bypass to Highest NRZ Rate is supported) must:\n  - Send EQ TS1 Ordered Sets with `speed_change` = 1b advertising:\n    - 8.0 GT/s Data Rate Identifier (for 8.0 GT/s redo).\n    - 32.0 GT/s Data Rate Identifier (for 32.0 GT/s redo).\n  - Ensure Data Rate is 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s before initiating the attempt (hardware or software initiated).\n- A **Downstream Port** intending to redo equalization from 8.0 GT/s → 16.0 GT/s, 16.0 GT/s → 32.0 GT/s, or 32.0 GT/s → 64.0 GT/s must:\n  - Send TS1 Ordered Sets with the **Equalization Redo** bit = 1b, `speed_change` = 1b, and the target Data Rate Identifier (16.0, 32.0, or 64.0 GT/s).\n  - Ensure Data Rate before initiating (hardware or software): 8.0 GT/s (for 16.0 GT/s redo), 16.0 GT/s (for 32.0 GT/s redo), 32.0 GT/s (for 64.0 GT/s redo).\n- An **Upstream Port** must advertise the highest data rate support in the TS2 Ordered Sets it transmits in Recovery.RcvrCfg (optionally in TS1 in this substate) if it receives EQ TS1/EQ TS2 with `speed_change` = 1b or TS1 with Equalization Redo bit = 1b or 128b/130b EQ TS2 with `speed_change` = 1b — unless the Upstream Port has determined a problem unrelated to highest data rate equalization prevents reliable operation.\n- After activating any inactive Lane, Transmitter must wait for TX common mode to settle before exiting Electrical Idle and transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets.\n  - Exception for L1.2 sub-state: common mode is permitted to be established passively during L1.0 and actively during Recovery. The Downstream Port must maintain a timer and must not send TS2 Ordered Sets until at least `T_COMMONMODE` has elapsed since it started transmitting TS1 Ordered Sets and detected electrical idle exit on any Lane.\n- If data rate is 8.0 GT/s or higher and `start_equalization_w_preset` = 1b:\n  - **Upstream Port** must use the Transmitter preset values registered from the appropriate eight consecutive EQ TS2 or 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets received in Recovery.RcvrCfg in its Transmitter setting as soon as it starts transmitting at the equalization data rate. Lanes with Reserved or unsupported preset values must use an implementation-specific supported Transmitter preset.\n  - **Downstream Port** must use Transmitter preset settings by the following priority:\n    1. If equalization data rate is 16.0/32.0/64.0 GT/s and eight consecutive EQ TS2 or 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets were received with supported preset values in the most recent Recovery.RcvrCfg transition — use those preset values.\n    2. Else if the Transmitter preset value in the appropriate Lane Equalization Control Register Entry is supported and not Reserved — use it (see table below for which register entry applies to each data rate).\n    3. Else use an implementation-specific supported Transmitter preset.\n\n    | Data Rate of Equalization | Transmitter Preset Source |\n    |---------------------------|---------------------------|\n    | 8.0 GT/s | Transmitter Preset field in Lane Equalization Control Register Entry. May optionally use Downstream Port 8.0 GT/s Receiver Preset Hint if not Reserved. |\n    | 16.0 GT/s | Downstream Port 16.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset field of 16.0 GT/s Lane Equalization Control Register Entry |\n    | 32.0 GT/s | Downstream Port 32.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset field of 32.0 GT/s Lane Equalization Control Register Entry |\n    | 64.0 GT/s | Downstream Port 64.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset field of 64.0 GT/s Lane Equalization Control Register Entry |\n\n  - Next state is **Recovery.Equalization**.\n- Else (`start_equalization_w_preset` = 0b or data rate < 8.0 GT/s):\n  - Transmitter must use the coefficient settings agreed upon at the conclusion of the last equalization procedure.\n  - If entered from Recovery.Equalization: Downstream Port must set Pre-cursor, Cursor, and Post-cursor Coefficient fields to current Transmitter settings in TS1 Ordered Sets; if the last accepted request in Phase 2 was a preset request, it must set Transmitter Preset bits to the accepted preset.\n  - It is recommended that all Ports set Pre-cursor, Cursor, Post-cursor Coefficient fields to current Transmitter settings and Transmitter Preset bits to the most recent preset in TS1 Ordered Sets.\n- Upstream Port must set the **Selectable De-emphasis** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) of TS1 Ordered Sets it transmits to match the desired de-emphasis level at 5.0 GT/s.\n- Downstream Port must ensure no more than 2 TS1 Ordered Sets with EC=00b are transmitted due to being in Recovery.RcvrLock before transitioning to Recovery.Equalization and starting to transmit TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- For data rate ≥ 8.0 GT/s: Receiver must consider any TS0, TS1, or TS2 Ordered Set to be received only after Block Alignment is obtained in that Lane.\n- **Upstream Port** must transition to **Recovery.Equalization** if it receives eight consecutive TS0 or TS1 Ordered Sets on all configured Lanes with all of the following:\n  - If TS1 Ordered Sets: Link and Lane numbers match the transmitted TS1 Ordered Sets on each Lane.\n  - If TS1 Ordered Sets: `speed_change` bit = 0b.\n  - If TS0 Ordered Sets: latest transition to Recovery.RcvrLock was from Recovery.Speed.\n  - If TS1 Ordered Sets: EC bits ≠ 00b.\n- Under other conditions, a device must not change the supported data rate values either in this substate or while in Recovery.RcvrCfg or Recovery.Equalization substates.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- **Transmit Margin** field of Link Control 2 Register is sampled on entry and becomes effective within **192 ns**.\n- If Downstream Port intends to use Upstream Port's de-emphasis info in Recovery.RcvrCfg: record **Selectable De-emphasis** bit received in this state.\n- `successful_speed_negotiation` is reset to 0b on entry.\n- `directed_speed_change` is set to 1b in L0 or L1 state for the side initiating a speed change; for the non-initiating side, it is set in this substate if received TS Ordered Sets have `speed_change` = 1b. It is reset to 0b in Recovery.Speed.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **Recovery.RcvrCfg** — eight consecutive TS1 or TS2 Ordered Sets received on all configured Lanes with matching Link and Lane numbers, `speed_change` equal to `directed_speed_change`, and EC field = 00b in all consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets if data rate ≥ 8.0 GT/s.\n  - If **Extended Synch** bit is set: Transmitter must send a minimum of 1024 consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets before transitioning to Recovery.RcvrCfg.\n  - If entered from Recovery.Equalization: Upstream Port must evaluate equalization coefficients or preset received by all Lanes (eight TS1 Ordered Sets) and note whether they differ from the final accepted coefficients/preset in Phase 2. Mismatches are reported in Recovery.RcvrCfg by setting the **Request Equalization** bit in TS2 Ordered Sets.\n- **Recovery.RcvrCfg** (after **24 ms** timeout) — if both of the following are true:\n  - One of: eight consecutive TS1 or TS2 received on any configured Lane with matching Link/Lane numbers and `speed_change` = 1b; or (Flit Mode) eight consecutive TS2s with Link number PAD and matching Lane number on any configured Lane.\n  - One of: current data rate > 2.5 GT/s; or highest data rate advertised in both transmitted TS1 and received TS1/TS2 is 5.0 GT/s or greater.\n- **Recovery.Speed** (after **24 ms** timeout) — if `changed_speed_recovery` = 0b and current speed > 2.5 GT/s. New rate after Recovery.Speed: 2.5 GT/s (8b/10b or 128b/130b) or 32.0 GT/s (1b/1b).\n- **Recovery.Speed** (after **24 ms** timeout) — if `changed_speed_recovery` = 1b. New rate after Recovery.Speed reverts to the speed at which Recovery was entered from L0 or L1.\n- **Configuration** (after **24 ms** timeout) — if all of the following are true:\n  - At least one configured Lane received at least one TS1 or TS2 with matching Link and Lane numbers.\n  - `changed_speed_recovery` = 0b.\n  - One of: `directed_speed_change` = 0b and `speed_change` = 0b in received TS1/TS2; or current data rate = 2.5 GT/s and 2.5 GT/s is the highest commonly advertised data rate.\n  - `directed_speed_change` is reset to 0b.\n- **Detect** — otherwise (after 24 ms timeout, none of the above conditions met).\n\nNotes:\n- Implementations must note that voltage levels may change after early bit lock and Symbol or Block alignment since the new Transmit Margin field becomes effective within 192 ns after the other side enters Recovery.RcvrLock. The Receiver needs to reacquire bit lock and Symbol or Block alignment under those conditions.\n- `directed_speed_change` is set to 1b in L0 or L1 for the initiating side; for the non-initiating side it is set in this substate if received TS Ordered Sets have `speed_change` set. It is reset in Recovery.Speed.\n- A device must accept all good TLPs and DLLPs received after entering this substate from L0 prior to receiving the first TS1 Ordered Set.\n- Under other conditions, a device must not change the supported data rate values either in this substate or while in Recovery.RcvrCfg or Recovery.Equalization substates.\n- If an Upstream Port not advertising 8.0 GT/s Data Rate Identifiers receives EQ TSs with 8.0 GT/s identifiers and `speed_change` set in Recovery.RcvrLock, this indicates the Downstream Port is attempting to switch to 8.0 GT/s for Link Equalization. If the Upstream Port is temporarily unable to advertise 8.0 GT/s in Recovery.RcvrCfg and does not intend to prohibit 8.0 GT/s operation, it should either: (a) enter Recovery, advertise 8.0 GT/s with `speed_change` = 1b, and if Recovery.Equalization is not entered, set **Request Equalization** and **Quiesce Guarantee** bits to 1b in TS2 at 8.0 GT/s in Recovery.RcvrCfg; or (b) enter Recovery and advertise 8.0 GT/s with `speed_change` = 0b. The same logic applies to 16.0/32.0/64.0 GT/s (using 128b/130b EQ TS2s).\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Equalization": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.2",
    sourceFile: "recovery.equalization.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.2\n\nDescription:\n- If entered from **Recovery.RcvrLock**: Transmitter sends TS0 or TS1 Ordered Sets on all configured Lanes (per Table 4-61) using the same Link and Lane numbers set after leaving Configuration. Receiver must consider any TS1 or TS0 Ordered Set received only after obtaining Block Alignment in that Lane.\n- If entered from **Loopback.Entry**:\n  - Transmitter sends TS0 or TS1 Ordered Sets (per Table 4-61) on all Lanes that detected a Receiver during Detect, using the Link and Lane numbers defined in Loopback.Entry.\n  - The Lane under test is the only Lane that participates in the equalization procedure.\n  - Lanes not under test must not be included in the equalization procedure; received data from them may be ignored. Lanes not under test must have their Transmitter preset values set to P4 / Q0. Their sole purpose is to create the noise needed in Loopback.Active.\n\nTable 4-61: Use of TS0 or TS1 Ordered Sets in different phases:\n\n| Current Data Rate / Port | Phase 0/1 | Phase 2 | Phase 3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 8.0 GT/s, 16.0 GT/s, or 32.0 GT/s; Up/Downstream | TS1 | TS1 | TS1 |\n| 64.0 GT/s Downstream Lanes | TS0 | TS0 followed by TS1 | TS1 |\n| 64.0 GT/s Upstream Lanes | TS0 | TS0 | TS0 followed by TS1 |\n\nEntry Actions:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Current phase is **Phase 1**.\n- Clear the following bits for the applicable data rate upon entry:\n  - At 8.0 GT/s: Equalization 8.0 GT/s Phase 1/2/3 Successful, Link Equalization Request 8.0 GT/s, and Equalization 8.0 GT/s Complete bits (Link Status 2 Register); also clear **Perform Equalization** bit (Link Control 3 Register).\n  - At 16.0 GT/s: Equalization 16.0 GT/s Phase 1/2/3 Successful, Link Equalization Request 16.0 GT/s, and Equalization 16.0 GT/s Complete bits (16.0 GT/s Status Register); also clear **Perform Equalization** bit (Link Control 3 Register).\n  - At 32.0 GT/s: Equalization 32.0 GT/s Phase 1/2/3 Successful, Link Equalization Request 32.0 GT/s, and Equalization 32.0 GT/s Complete bits (32.0 GT/s Status Register); also clear **Perform Equalization** bit.\n  - At 64.0 GT/s: Equalization 64.0 GT/s Phase 1/2/3 Successful, Link Equalization Request 64.0 GT/s, and Equalization 64.0 GT/s Complete bits (64.0 GT/s Status Register); also clear **Perform Equalization** bit.\n- Set `equalization_done_<rate>_data_rate` variable to 1b for the applicable data rate.\n- `start_equalization_w_preset` is set to 0b.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Current phase is **Phase 0**.\n- Clear the following bits for the applicable data rate upon entry (same registers as Downstream, but **Perform Equalization** bit in Link Control 3 Register is NOT cleared):\n  - At 8.0/16.0/32.0/64.0 GT/s: corresponding Phase 1/2/3 Successful, Link Equalization Request, and Complete bits.\n- Set `equalization_done_<rate>_data_rate` variable to 1b for the applicable data rate.\n- `start_equalization_w_preset` is set to 0b.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes — Phase 1\n- Transmitter sends TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets using the current Transmitter preset settings. EC field = 01b (for TS0, initially 00b; after two consecutive TS0 with Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b received, set EC = 01b). TS0/TS1 Transmitter Preset bits = current Transmitter preset for the data rate. FS and LF fields set to appropriate values. Post-cursor Coefficient field (TS1) = value corresponding to Transmitter Preset bits.\n- Transmitter Preset settings must be chosen as follows (in priority order):\n  1. If entered from **Loopback.Entry** with EQ TS1 Ordered Sets directing to Loopback.Entry: use the Transmitter preset specified in the Preset field of those EQ TS1 Ordered Sets (Lane under test). If `perform_equalization_for_loopback_64GT` = 1b: advertise 64.0 GT/s support in TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets (Data Rate Identifier must use Flit Mode Encoding).\n  2. If entered from **Loopback.Entry** with standard TS1 Ordered Sets: use an implementation-specific supported Transmitter preset value.\n  3. Else if eight consecutive 128b/130b EQ TS2 with supported preset values were received in the most recent Recovery.RcvrCfg and current data rate ≥ 16.0 GT/s: use the preset requested in those 128b/130b EQ TS2.\n  4. Else if eight consecutive EQ TS2 with supported preset values were received in the most recent Recovery.RcvrCfg and current rate = 32.0 GT/s and equalization bypass to 32.0 GT/s is being performed: use those preset values.\n  5. Else if the Transmitter preset in the appropriate Lane Equalization Control Register Entry is supported and not Reserved: use it (8.0 GT/s → Downstream Port Transmitter Preset field; 16.0 → 16.0 GT/s LECE; 32.0 → 32.0 GT/s LECE; 64.0 → 64.0 GT/s LECE).\n  6. Else use an implementation-specific supported Transmitter preset.\n\n### Downstream Lanes — Phase 2\n- Transmitter sends TS0 with EC=10b if data rate = 64.0 GT/s and not all Lanes have received two consecutive TS0 with EC=10b since entering Phase 2; otherwise sends TS1 Ordered Sets.\n- Transmitter sends TS1 with EC=10b and coefficient settings per Lane as follows:\n  - If two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=10b have been received since entering Phase 2 (or since the last different two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=10b):\n    - If the preset or coefficients requested are legal and supported: change Transmitter settings so new settings are effective at pins within **500 ns** of end of second TS0/TS1 requesting the setting (change must not cause illegal voltage for more than 1 ns). Reflect: Transmitter Preset bits = requested preset (for preset request); Pre-cursor/Cursor/Post-cursor fields = Transmitter settings; Reject Coefficient Values bit = clear.\n    - Else (requested preset/coefficients illegal or unsupported): do not change Transmitter settings; reflect requested preset/coefficient values in TS1; set **Reject Coefficient Values** bit = 1b.\n  - Else: reflect the preset and coefficients currently in use by the Transmitter.\n\n### Downstream Lanes — Phase 3\n- Transmitter sends TS1 with EC=11b.\n- Port must evaluate and arrive at optimal settings independently per Lane. When `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b, equalization is only performed on the Lane under test.\n- To evaluate a new preset or coefficient setting:\n  - Request a new preset (set Transmitter Preset bits + Use Preset = 1b) or new coefficients (set Pre-cursor/Cursor/Post-cursor + Use Preset = 0b). Request must be continuously made for at least **1 µs** or until evaluation completes, whichever is later.\n  - Wait the required time (500 ns + roundtrip delay including logic delays) to ensure the Upstream Port is transmitting using requested settings if accepted. Obtain Block Alignment and evaluate incoming Ordered Sets.\n  - Downstream Port is responsible for setting the **Reset EIEOS Interval Count** bit in TS1 according to its evaluation criteria. The **Use Preset** bit of received TS1 must not be used to determine accept/reject.\n  - At ≤ 32.0 GT/s: total time per preset/coefficient request (from transmission to evaluation completion) must be < **2 ms**. May extend beyond 2 ms for the final optimization stage, but must adhere to the phase timeout (**24 ms** for 8.0/16.0/32.0 GT/s; **48 ms** for 64.0 GT/s), and this exception must not be taken more than twice.\n  - At ≥ 64.0 GT/s: may evaluate each request for an arbitrary time; must still adhere to the phase timeout.\n  - All new preset/coefficient settings must be presented on all configured Lanes simultaneously. Any Lane may continue transmitting its current setting as its new value if it does not want to change at that time.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 0\n- If entered from **Loopback.Entry**: send TS0/TS1 with EC=00b, Transmitter Preset bits = current value. Pre-cursor/Cursor/Post-cursor (TS1) = values corresponding to Transmitter Preset.\n  - Transmitter Preset for Lane under test chosen as follows:\n    - If EQ TS1 Ordered Sets directed from Configuration.Linkwidth.Start to Loopback.Entry: use the preset specified in those EQ TS1 Ordered Sets.\n    - If standard TS1 Ordered Sets: use implementation-specific supported Transmitter preset.\n    - If TS0 Ordered Sets are transmitted: components are permitted (though not recommended) to use an implementation-specific supported Transmitter preset.\n- If current rate = 8.0 GT/s: send TS1 using Transmitter settings specified by preset bits received in EQ TS2 Ordered Sets during most recent transition to 8.0 GT/s from 2.5/5.0 GT/s.\n- If current rate = 16.0 GT/s: send TS1 using 16.0 GT/s Transmitter settings from 128b/130b EQ TS2 received during most recent transition to 16.0 GT/s from 8.0 GT/s.\n- If current rate = 32.0 GT/s and `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b: send TS1 using 32.0 GT/s Transmitter settings from the appropriate TS2 during most recent transition to 32.0 GT/s (EQ TS2 if equalization bypass; 128b/130b EQ TS2 if from 16.0 GT/s).\n- If current rate = 64.0 GT/s and `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b: send TS0 using 64.0 GT/s Transmitter settings from 128b/130b EQ TS2 at 32.0 GT/s during most recent transition to 64.0 GT/s.\n- Lanes with Reserved or unsupported Transmitter preset: use implementation-specific preset. In TS1/TS0: set Transmitter Preset field to received value; set **Reject Coefficient Values** (TS1) = 1b; set Coefficient fields to values corresponding to the implementation-specific preset.\n- Lanes that did not receive EQ TS2 or 128b/130b EQ TS2: in TS1/TS0: set Transmitter Preset to implementation-specific value; Reject Coefficient Values (TS1) = clear; Coefficient fields correspond to that preset.\n- In TS1 Ordered Sets: EC = 00b; Transmitter Preset bits = value received in EQ TS2 / 128b/130b EQ TS2; Pre-cursor/Cursor/Post-cursor = values corresponding to those Transmitter Preset bits.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 1\n- Transmitter sends TS0/TS1 with EC=01b using the Transmitter settings determined in Phase 0. FS, LF, and (TS1 only) Post-cursor Coefficient fields = values corresponding to current Transmitter settings.\n- If entered from **Loopback.Entry** and `perform_equalization_for_loopback_64GT` = 1b: Loopback Follower must advertise 64.0 GT/s support in TS0/TS1 (Data Rate Identifier must use Flit Mode Encoding).\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 2\n- Transmitter sends TS0/TS1 with EC=10b.\n- Port must evaluate and arrive at optimal settings independently per Lane. When `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b, equalization is only performed on the Lane under test.\n- Same evaluation rules as Downstream Phase 3 (request presets or coefficients, wait 500 ns + roundtrip delay, evaluate, use **Reset EIEOS Interval Count** bit).\n- At ≤ 32.0 GT/s: < **2 ms** per evaluation (same 2-exception rule applies). Timeout: **24 ms** (8.0/16.0/32.0 GT/s) or **48 ms** (64.0 GT/s).\n- At ≥ 64.0 GT/s: arbitrary evaluation time per request; must adhere to timeout.\n- All new settings must be presented on all configured Lanes simultaneously.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 3\n- Transmitter sends TS0 with EC=11b if data rate = 64.0 GT/s and not all Lanes have received two consecutive TS1 with EC=11b since entering Phase 3; otherwise sends TS1.\n- Transmitter sends TS0/TS1 with EC=11b and coefficient settings per Lane independently, using the same accept/reject logic as Downstream Phase 2:\n  - If two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=11b received (or a new set with different settings):\n    - Legal and supported: change Transmitter settings within **500 ns** (change must not cause illegal voltage for more than 1 ns). Reflect preset/coefficients; Reject Coefficient Values = clear.\n    - Illegal or unsupported: reflect requested preset/coefficients; Reject Coefficient Values = 1b.\n  - Else: reflect current preset and coefficients.\n- The Transmitter preset initially transmitted on entry to Phase 3 may be the preset transmitted in Phase 0 for the same data rate, or the Transmitter preset currently in use.\n\nRx Expectations:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- **Phase 1**: Downstream Port is permitted to wait up to **500 ns** after entering Phase 1 before evaluating received TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets (to stabilize Receiver logic).\n- **Phase 1 → Phase 2**: Receiver must complete bit lock and recognize Ordered Sets within **2 ms** after receiving the first bit of the first valid Ordered Set.\n- **Phase 1 → Phase 2**: LF and FS values from two consecutive TS0/TS1 must be stored for use in Phase 3 (if Downstream Port wants to adjust Upstream Port's Transmitter coefficients).\n- **Phase 3**: If two consecutive TS1 with the requested Transmitter Preset or Pre-cursor/Cursor/Post-cursor fields identical to the request and Reject Coefficient Values = clear → setting accepted; evaluate as candidate final setting.\n- **Phase 3**: If two consecutive TS1 with the requested settings and Reject Coefficient Values = 1b → setting rejected; must not be a candidate final setting.\n- **Phase 3**: If after an implementation-specific time following start of evaluation, no consecutive TS1 matching the request → setting must not be a candidate final setting.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- **Phase 0**: Upstream Port is permitted to wait up to **500 ns** after entering Phase 0 before evaluating TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets.\n- **Phase 0 → Phase 1**: Receiver must complete bit lock and recognize Ordered Sets within **2 ms** after receiving the first bit of the first valid Ordered Set.\n- **Phase 0 → Phase 1**: LF and FS values from two consecutive TS0/TS1 must be stored for use in Phase 2 (if Upstream Port wants to adjust Downstream Port's Transmitter coefficients).\n- **Phase 2**: Same accept/reject rules as Downstream Phase 3 — matching requested Transmitter Preset or coefficient fields in two consecutive TS1, with Reject Coefficient Values bit determining accepted/rejected status.\n\nRegister Updates:\n\n### Downstream Lanes — Phase 1 Transitions\n- **→ Phase 2**: Set Equalization `<rate>` GT/s Phase 1 Successful bit (for applicable rate register).\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock** (skip Phases 2 & 3): Set Equalization Phase 1 Successful, Phase 2 Successful, Phase 3 Successful, and Complete bits for applicable data rate. At 64.0 GT/s: Transmitter must send 24 TS0 with EC=00b prior to transitioning.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (24 ms timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit for applicable data rate; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\n### Downstream Lanes — Phase 2 Transitions\n- **→ Phase 3**: Set Equalization `<rate>` GT/s Phase 2 Successful bit.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\n### Downstream Lanes — Phase 3 Transitions\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock**: Set Equalization Phase 3 Successful and Complete bits for applicable data rate.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 0 Transitions\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (12 ms timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 1 Transitions\n- **→ Phase 2**: Set Equalization `<rate>` GT/s Phase 1 Successful bit.\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock** (8 consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=00b received): Set Equalization Phase 1 Successful and Complete bits.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (12 ms timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 2 Transitions\n- **→ Phase 3**: Set Equalization `<rate>` GT/s Phase 2 Successful bit.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\n### Upstream Lanes — Phase 3 Transitions\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock**: Set Equalization Phase 3 Successful and Complete bits.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed** (timeout): Set Equalization Complete bit; `successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n\n**Phase 1:**\n- **→ Phase 2**: All configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=01b AND Downstream Port wants to execute Phase 2 and Phase 3. (When `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b and Flit Mode Supported = 1b, Phase 2 and Phase 3 must be executed.)\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock**: All configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=01b AND `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b AND Downstream Port does not want to execute Phase 2 and Phase 3.\n- **→ Loopback.Entry**: **24 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **24 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\n**Phase 2:**\n- **→ Phase 3**: All configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=11b.\n- **→ Loopback.Entry**: **32 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms) for data rates < 64.0 GT/s if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b. **64 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms) at 64.0 GT/s if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **32 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms) if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b and rate < 64.0 GT/s. **64 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms) if rate = 64.0 GT/s.\n\n**Phase 3:**\n- **→ Loopback.Entry** (32.0 GT/s, `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b): Lane under test at optimal setting AND two consecutive TS1 with Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b received, OR **24 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms).\n- **→ Loopback.Entry** (64.0 GT/s, `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b): Lane under test at optimal setting AND all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b, OR **48 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms).\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock**: `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b AND all configured Lanes at optimal setting AND either rate = 8.0 GT/s OR all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **24 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms) if rate < 64.0 GT/s. **48 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms) if rate = 64.0 GT/s.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n\n**Phase 0:**\n- **→ Phase 1**: All configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with EC=01b, OR all configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS0 with EC=01b and Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b.\n- **→ Loopback.Entry**: **12 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **12 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\n**Phase 1:**\n- **→ Phase 2**: All configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=10b.\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock**: All configured Lanes receive eight consecutive TS0/TS1 with EC=00b AND `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n- **→ Loopback.Entry**: **12 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **12 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\n**Phase 2:**\n- **→ Phase 3**: `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b AND Lane under test at optimal setting AND two consecutive TS1 with Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b received.\n- **→ Phase 3**: `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b AND all configured Lanes at optimal settings AND either rate = 8.0 GT/s OR all Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b.\n- **→ Loopback.Entry**: **24 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms) if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b and rate < 64.0 GT/s. **48 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms) if rate = 64.0 GT/s.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **24 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms) if rate < 64.0 GT/s. **48 ms** timeout (-0/+2 ms) if rate = 64.0 GT/s.\n\n**Phase 3:**\n- **→ Loopback.Entry** (rate < 64.0 GT/s, `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b): Lane under test receives two consecutive TS1 with EC=00b, OR **32 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms).\n- **→ Loopback.Entry** (rate = 64.0 GT/s, `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b): Lane under test receives two consecutive TS1 with EC=00b, OR **64 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms).\n- **→ Recovery.RcvrLock**: All configured Lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with EC=00b.\n- **→ Recovery.Speed**: **32 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms) if rate < 64.0 GT/s. **64 ms** timeout (-0/+4 ms) if rate = 64.0 GT/s.\n\nNotes:\n- **IMPLEMENTATION NOTE — TS0 TO TS1 TRANSITIONS:** All TS0 → TS1 transitions are expected and initiated by a Port so that its Receiver is prepared for the NRZ to PAM4 transition. The first transition occurs for Downstream Lanes when entering Phase 2: Downstream Lanes send TS0 with EC=00b until they reach a target BER of 1E-4, then send EC=01b, then wait to receive EC=10b from the Upstream Port. When Upstream Port sends EC=10b, the transition is guaranteed within a fixed time. The other transition happens during Phase 3 for Upstream Lanes when the Upstream Port receives EC=11b. A provision is made for Retimers to request extended EQ during Phase 0 & 1 for 64.0 GT/s equalization.\n- **IMPLEMENTATION NOTE — RESET EIEOS AND COEFFICIENT/PRESET REQUESTS (Downstream Phase 3 / Upstream Phase 2):** A Port may set **Reset EIEOS Interval Count** to 1b when it wants a longer PRBS pattern and subsequently clear it when it needs to obtain Block Alignment. All TS1 Ordered Sets in Phase 3 (Downstream) or Phase 2 (Upstream) are requests. The first request may be a new preset, a new coefficient request, or a request to maintain the current link partner transmitter settings by reflecting the settings from the two consecutive TS1 with EC=11b (Phase 3) or EC=10b (Phase 2) that caused the phase transition.\n- **IMPLEMENTATION NOTE — REJECT COEFFICIENT VALUES WITH TS0 ORDERED SETS:** The **Reject Coefficient Values** bit is intentionally omitted from the TS0 Ordered Set definition. As a result, Upstream Lanes cannot explicitly indicate that a Reserved or unsupported Transmitter preset value was received in 128b/130b EQ TS2 at 32.0 GT/s. To determine whether an Upstream Lane is using said Transmitter Preset, the Downstream Lane is permitted to observe the Transmitter Preset setting in TS0 Ordered Sets received in Recovery.Equalization Phase 1 with EC field = 00b or 01b.\n- A transition to Recovery.RcvrLock from Downstream Phase 1 may be used when the Downstream Port determines that Phase 2 and Phase 3 are not needed based on platform and channel characteristics.\n- If the requester is unable to receive Ordered Sets within the timeout period during Phase 3 (Downstream) or Phase 2 (Upstream), it may assume that the requested setting does not work in that Lane.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Equalization.Phase0": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.2.2.1",
    sourceFile: "recovery.equalization.phase0.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.2.2.1\n\nDescription:\n- **Phase 0** is used by Upstream Lanes in Recovery.Equalization to establish starting transmitter settings before entering full equalization exchange.\n- Ordered Set type follows data-rate rules from Table 4-61 (TS1 at 8.0/16.0/32.0 GT/s, TS0 at 64.0 GT/s where applicable).\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Current phase for Upstream Lanes is Phase 0 when entering Recovery.Equalization.\n- For the active data rate, clear Equalization status bits for Phase 1/2/3, Link Equalization Request, and Complete in the corresponding status register.\n- Set the corresponding `equalization_done_<rate>_data_rate` tracking variable as defined for Recovery.Equalization entry handling.\n- Set `start_equalization_w_preset` to 0b.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- Upstream transmitter sends TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets with **EC = 00b** using the initial preset selection rules:\n  - If entered from Loopback.Entry, use loopback-specific preset selection rules.\n  - Else use the most recently negotiated preset information from Recovery.RcvrCfg (EQ TS2 / 128b/130b EQ TS2) when available.\n  - If received preset is unsupported/reserved, use an implementation-specific supported preset and reflect reject behavior per rules.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Upstream receiver may wait up to **500 ns** before evaluating received Ordered Sets to stabilize logic.\n- Receiver must complete lock/recognition requirements before phase transition criteria are evaluated.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- Phase-success and completion bits are updated only when transitioning out of Phase 0 per timing/exit criteria.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **Recovery.Equalization.Phase1.Upstream** — all configured lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with **EC = 01b**, or TS0 with **EC = 01b** and Retimer Equalization Extend bit = 0b.\n- **Loopback.Entry** — **12 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **Recovery.Speed** — **12 ms** timeout if `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\nNotes:\n- Phase 0 exists only on the Upstream equalization path.\n- At 64.0 GT/s, TS0/TS1 transitions must follow retimer and alignment criteria exactly before advancing.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Equalization.Phase1": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.2.1.1 / 4.2.7.4.2.2.2",
    sourceFile: "recovery.equalization.phase1.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.2.1.1 / 4.2.7.4.2.2.2\n\nDescription:\n- **Phase 1** establishes stable transmitter operation and prepares each lane for coefficient/preset adjustment phases.\n- This phase exists on both Downstream and Upstream equalization paths.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Downstream path enters Recovery.Equalization in Phase 1.\n- Required equalization status bits for the active data rate are initialized on entry.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Send TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets with **EC = 01b** using selected preset rules for the current data rate.\n- At 64.0 GT/s, TS0 sequencing and EC progression must follow retimer-extend conditions before normal TS1 behavior.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Send TS0/TS1 Ordered Sets with **EC = 01b** using the transmitter settings established by Phase 0.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Receiver must satisfy lock/alignment timing requirements before advancing.\n- LF/FS values from consecutive Ordered Sets are stored for later phase evaluation where required.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- On successful Phase 1 completion, set the corresponding **Phase 1 Successful** bit for the active data rate.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream\n- **Recovery.Equalization.Phase2.Downstream** — all configured lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with **EC = 01b** and Downstream chooses to execute Phases 2 and 3.\n- **Recovery.RcvrLock** — all configured lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with **EC = 01b** and Downstream chooses to skip Phases 2 and 3.\n- **Loopback.Entry** — **24 ms** timeout when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **Recovery.Speed** — **24 ms** timeout when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\n### Upstream\n- **Recovery.Equalization.Phase2.Upstream** — all configured lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with **EC = 10b**.\n- **Recovery.RcvrLock** — all configured lanes receive eight consecutive TS0/TS1 with **EC = 00b** and `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n- **Loopback.Entry** — **12 ms** timeout when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b.\n- **Recovery.Speed** — **12 ms** timeout when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\nNotes:\n- Whether to continue into full tuning (Phases 2 and 3) is explicitly controlled by phase-exit conditions, not just data rate.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Equalization.Phase2": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.2.1.2 / 4.2.7.4.2.2.3",
    sourceFile: "recovery.equalization.phase2.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.2.1.2 / 4.2.7.4.2.2.3\n\nDescription:\n- **Phase 2** performs active preset/coefficients tuning exchange.\n- Request/response validity is evaluated lane-by-lane under timing constraints.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Phase entry occurs after Phase 1 success criteria are met for the corresponding direction.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Lanes\n- Send TS0/TS1 with **EC = 10b** (64.0 GT/s TS0 handling applies until TS1 criteria are satisfied).\n- If requested preset/coefficients are legal and supported, update transmitter settings so they are effective within **500 ns** of request-complete timing.\n- If illegal/unsupported, reflect request and assert reject behavior without applying unsupported setting.\n\n### Upstream Lanes\n- Send TS0/TS1 with **EC = 10b** and evaluate tuning requests similarly.\n- Apply request handling and reject behavior according to legality/support rules.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Acceptance/rejection of requested preset/coefficients is inferred from consecutive received Ordered Sets and reject signaling behavior.\n- Evaluation window per request at <= 32.0 GT/s is bounded, with limited exception allowance, while overall phase timeout limits still apply.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- On successful Phase 2 completion, set the corresponding **Phase 2 Successful** bit for the active data rate.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream\n- **Recovery.Equalization.Phase3.Downstream** — all configured lanes receive two consecutive TS0/TS1 with **EC = 11b**.\n- **Loopback.Entry** — timeout path:\n  - **32 ms** for data rates < 64.0 GT/s\n  - **64 ms** for 64.0 GT/s\n  (when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b)\n- **Recovery.Speed** — same timeout values when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\n### Upstream\n- **Recovery.Equalization.Phase3.Upstream** — lane-under-test or all configured lanes meet optimal-setting/retimer criteria.\n- **Loopback.Entry** — timeout path:\n  - **24 ms** for data rates < 64.0 GT/s\n  - **48 ms** for 64.0 GT/s\n  (when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 1b)\n- **Recovery.Speed** — same timeout values when `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b.\n\nNotes:\n- All-lane simultaneous presentation requirements still apply even when only one lane is actively retuned in a given evaluation step.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Equalization.Phase3": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.2.1.3 / 4.2.7.4.2.2.4",
    sourceFile: "recovery.equalization.phase3.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.2.1.3 / 4.2.7.4.2.2.4\n\nDescription:\n- **Phase 3** finalizes equalization and exits to Recovery.RcvrLock on success or timeout exits on failure/loopback flows.\n- This phase is present on both Downstream and Upstream equalization paths.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- Enter after Phase 2 success conditions for the corresponding direction.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- Transmitters send TS0/TS1 with **EC = 11b** behavior per data rate and lane criteria.\n- Requested settings are validated through consecutive Ordered Set exchange and reject signaling rules.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Receiver confirms accepted/rejected requests based on consecutive matching Ordered Sets and reject indication.\n- If expected accepted-pattern response is not observed within implementation-specific evaluation time, that request is treated as non-viable.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- On successful completion, set corresponding **Phase 3 Successful** and **Equalization Complete** bits for the active data rate.\n- Timeout exits set **Equalization Complete** and clear `successful_speed_negotiation` as defined by Recovery.Equalization timeout flow.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n\n### Downstream\n- **Recovery.RcvrLock** — `perform_equalization_for_loopback` = 0b and all configured lanes meet optimal-setting criteria (including required retimer/TS1 criteria when applicable).\n- **Loopback.Entry** — loopback flow criteria met, or timeout path:\n  - **24 ms** for rates < 64.0 GT/s\n  - **48 ms** for 64.0 GT/s\n- **Recovery.Speed** — same timeout path when equalization does not complete successfully.\n\n### Upstream\n- **Recovery.RcvrLock** — all configured lanes receive two consecutive TS1 with **EC = 00b**.\n- **Loopback.Entry** — lane-under-test criteria met, or timeout path:\n  - **32 ms** for rates < 64.0 GT/s\n  - **64 ms** for 64.0 GT/s\n- **Recovery.Speed** — same timeout path when completion criteria are not met.\n\nNotes:\n- Phase 3 is where final accepted settings are locked in before re-entering Recovery.RcvrLock normal flow.\n- Timeout path thresholds differ between Upstream and Downstream paths; preserve direction-specific limits.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Speed": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.3",
    sourceFile: "recovery.speed.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.3\n\nDescription:\n- Transmitter enters Electrical Idle and remains there until all Receiver Lanes have also entered Electrical Idle, then stays there for an additional minimum period depending on speed negotiation outcome:\n  - **Successful speed negotiation** (`successful_speed_negotiation` = 1b): at least **800 ns** but no longer than an additional **1 ms**.\n  - **Unsuccessful speed negotiation** (`successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b): at least **6 µs** but no longer than an additional **1 ms**.\n- Frequency of operation may only be changed to the new data rate after the Receiver Lanes have entered Electrical Idle.\n- If the negotiated data rate is **5.0 GT/s** and operating in full-swing mode:\n  - Select **-6 dB** de-emphasis if `select_deemphasis` = 0b.\n  - Select **-3.5 dB** de-emphasis if `select_deemphasis` = 1b.\n- If the Link is already operating at the highest data rate supported by both Ports, Recovery.Speed is executed but the data rate is not changed.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- An **EIOSQ** must be sent prior to entering Electrical Idle.\n- On entry following a **successful** speed negotiation (`successful_speed_negotiation` = 1b): an Electrical Idle condition may be inferred on the Receiver Lanes if a TS1 or TS2 Ordered Set has not been received on any configured Lane within the interval specified in Table 4-48.\n- On entry following an **unsuccessful** speed negotiation (`successful_speed_negotiation` = 0b): Electrical Idle may be inferred if an exit from Electrical Idle has not been detected at least once on any configured Lane within the interval specified in Table 4-48.\n\nTx Behavior:\n- Transmitter enters and holds Electrical Idle as described in Description above.\n- De-emphasis selection at 5.0 GT/s full-swing mode is applied as described in Description above.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- Electrical Idle condition exists on the Lanes if an EIOS is received on any configured Lane, or Electrical Idle is detected/inferred as described in Section 4.2.5.4.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- `directed_speed_change` is reset to 0b.\n- The new data rate is reflected in the **Current Link Speed** field of the Link Status Register.\n- On a Link bandwidth change:\n  - If `successful_speed_negotiation` = 1b **and** the **Autonomous Change** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) in the eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets received in Recovery.RcvrCfg is 1b, or the speed change was initiated by the Downstream Port for autonomous reasons: the **Link Autonomous Bandwidth Status** bit of the Link Status Register is set to 1b.\n  - Otherwise: the **Link Bandwidth Management Status** bit of the Link Status Register is set to 1b.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **Recovery.RcvrLock** — after Transmitter Lanes are no longer required to be in Electrical Idle (per conditions above). New data rate is applied based on the following priority:\n  1. `successful_speed_negotiation` = 1b: new data rate = highest common rate advertised by both sides. `changed_speed_recovery` is set to 1b.\n  2. Entering Recovery.Speed a second time since entering Recovery from L0 or L1 (`changed_speed_recovery` = 1b): new data rate = the rate at which LTSSM entered Recovery from L0 or L1. `changed_speed_recovery` is reset to 0b.\n  3. Latest encoding before entering Recovery.Speed was 1b/1b: new data rate = **32.0 GT/s**. `changed_speed_recovery` remains 0b.\n  4. Otherwise: new data rate = **2.5 GT/s**. `changed_speed_recovery` remains 0b.\n- **Detect** — after a **48 ms** timeout if Link Speed at entry was < 64.0 GT/s. *(Not expected under normal conditions.)*\n- **Detect** — after a **96 ms** timeout (strongly recommended) or **48 ms** timeout if Link Speed at entry was ≥ 64.0 GT/s. *(96 ms recommended to avoid unintended timeout caused by 64.0 GT/s Recovery.Equalization Phase 2 timeout of 64 ms being larger than Phase 1 timeout (12 ms) + Recovery.Speed timeout (48 ms).)*\n\nNotes:\n- The DC common mode voltage is not required to be within specification while in Electrical Idle in this substate.\n- The 96 ms timeout at 64.0 GT/s is strongly recommended to prevent premature timeout to Detect.\n- The timeout case at lower speeds represents the scenario where the frequency of operation in L0 was > 2.5 GT/s and one side could not operate at that frequency, timing out in Recovery.RcvrLock on the first entry from L0 or L1.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.RcvrCfg": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.4",
    sourceFile: "recovery.rcvrcfg.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.4\n\nDescription:\n- In Non-Flit Mode: Transmitter sends TS2 Ordered Sets on all configured Lanes using the same Link and Lane numbers set after leaving Configuration.\n- In Flit Mode: Transmitter sends TS2 Ordered Sets on all configured Lanes with the Link number field set as follows — if the LTSSM is initiating a Link width change by transitioning to Configuration from this state and the Lane will be removed from the Link, the Link number field is set to PAD; otherwise it is set to the Link number.\n- In 1b/1b TS2 Ordered Sets, if an equalization request is being communicated: Symbols 1,9 represent Equalization Byte 0 (instead of Link Number), the **Request Equalization** bit is set to 1b, and other fields are set as described in Section 4.2.4 and Table 4-37.\n- The LTSSM must not initiate a width change if `speed_change` = 1b or `directed_speed_change` is already 1b.\n- `N_FTS` in the transmitted TS2 Ordered Sets should reflect the number at the current data rate for Non-Flit Mode.\n- In Flit Mode, the appropriate Training Control bit of the TS2 Ordered Set must be set if the Port intends to drive the Link to Hot Reset, Disabled, or Loopback immediately after exiting this sub-state.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- `start_equalization_w_preset` is reset to 0b upon entry.\n- On entry, a **Downstream Port** must set `select_deemphasis` equal to the **Selectable De-emphasis** field in the Link Control 2 Register, or adopt an implementation-specific mechanism (including using the value requested by the Upstream Port in the eight consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets it received).\n- A **Downstream Port** advertising 5.0 GT/s support must set the **Selectable De-emphasis** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) of transmitted TS2 Ordered Sets identical to `select_deemphasis`.\n- An **Upstream Port** must set its **Autonomous Change** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) to 1b in TS2 Ordered Sets if it intends to change the Link bandwidth for autonomous reasons.\n\nTx Behavior:\n\n### Downstream Port\n- Must transmit EQ TS2 Ordered Sets (Symbol 6 bit 7 = 1b) per Lane with the **Transmitter Preset** and **Receiver Preset Hint** fields from the corresponding Lane Equalization Control Register Entry if **all** of the following are satisfied (for 8.0 GT/s):\n  - Downstream Port advertised 8.0 GT/s support in Recovery.RcvrLock, and 8.0 GT/s support was advertised in Configuration.Complete or Recovery.RcvrCfg by the Upstream Port since exiting Detect, and eight consecutive TS1 or TS2 Ordered Sets were received on any configured Lane prior to entry with `speed_change` bit = 1b.\n  - `equalization_done_8GT_data_rate` = 0b, or the **Perform Equalization** bit in Link Control 3 Register is set, or an implementation-specific mechanism determined equalization needs to be performed.\n  - Current data rate is 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s.\n- Must transmit EQ TS2 Ordered Sets with the **32.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset** bits if all analogous conditions are satisfied for 32.0 GT/s (current rate 16.0 GT/s, `equalization_done_32GT_data_rate` = 0b or directed).\n- Must transmit 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets with the **16.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset** bits if all analogous conditions are satisfied for 16.0 GT/s (current rate 8.0 GT/s, `equalization_done_16GT_data_rate` = 0b or directed).\n- Must transmit 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets with the **64.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset** bits if all analogous conditions are satisfied for 64.0 GT/s (current rate 32.0 GT/s, `equalization_done_64GT_data_rate` = 0b or directed).\n- Must record the 16.0/32.0/64.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset settings advertised in eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets received if they are 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets and the corresponding data rate support is advertised by both ends.\n- If the Link is already operating at the highest data rate supported by both Ports, Recovery.Speed is executed but the data rate is not changed.\n- If 128b/130b or 1b/1b encoding is used and the **Request Equalization** bit is set in eight consecutive received TS2 Ordered Sets, the Port must handle it as an equalization request per Section 4.2.4.\n\n### Upstream Port\n- Is permitted to transmit 8b/10b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets (for equalization bypass to 32.0 GT/s) with implementation-specific 32.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset bits if all conditions are satisfied (rate 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s, equalization bypass to highest NRZ rate negotiated, etc.).\n- Is permitted to transmit 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets with 32.0/64.0 GT/s Transmitter Preset bits under analogous conditions for 32.0 GT/s (rate 16.0 GT/s) or 64.0 GT/s (rate 32.0 GT/s).\n- Any configured Lanes which do not receive appropriate EQ TS2 or 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets will use implementation-dependent preset values when first operating at 8.0, 16.0, 32.0, or 64.0 GT/s prior to performing link equalization.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- **Recovery.Speed** — if all of the following are true:\n  - One of the following:\n    - Eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets (standard) received on any configured Lane with identical data rate identifiers, identical Symbol 6, and `speed_change` = 1b (8b/10b or 128b/130b encoding).\n    - Eight consecutive EQ TS2 or 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets received on all configured Lanes with identical data rate identifiers, identical Symbol 6, and `speed_change` = 1b.\n    - Eight consecutive EQ or 128b/130b EQ TS2 Ordered Sets received on any configured Lane with identical data rate identifiers, identical Symbol 6, `speed_change` = 1b, and **1 ms** has elapsed since receipt of the eight consecutive EQ Ordered Sets.\n    - Eight consecutive and identical TS2 Ordered Sets received on any configured Lane with `speed_change` = 1b (1b/1b encoding).\n  - Either current data rate > 2.5 GT/s, or > 2.5 GT/s data rate identifiers are set in both transmitted and received TS2 Ordered Sets.\n  - For 8b/10b: at least 32 TS2 Ordered Sets (without interruption by EIEOS) transmitted with `speed_change` = 1b after receiving one TS2 with `speed_change` = 1b. For 128b/130b and 1b/1b: at least 128 TS2 Ordered Sets.\n- **Configuration** (Non-Flit Mode) — eight consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets received on any configured Lane with Link or Lane numbers not matching what is being transmitted, and 16 TS2 Ordered Sets sent after receiving one TS1 Ordered Set (8b/10b or 128b/130b encoding), with one of the following:\n  - `speed_change` bit = 0b in received TS1 Ordered Sets.\n  - Current data rate is 2.5 GT/s and no 5.0 GT/s (or higher) data rate identifiers in received TS1 or transmitted TS2 Ordered Sets.\n- **Configuration** (Flit Mode) — if all of the following are true:\n  - **1 ms** has elapsed after receiving one TS1 or TS2 Ordered Set in any configured Lane.\n  - One of: eight consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets after receiving a TS2; or eight consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets with Link/Lane number PAD and no TS2 received; or eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with Link number PAD on any configured Lane or PAD is being transmitted.\n  - At least 16 TS2 Ordered Sets transmitted after receiving one TS1 with Link/Lane number PAD or one TS2 Ordered Set.\n  - `speed_change` bit = 0b in received TS1 or TS2 Ordered Sets.\n- **Recovery.Speed** — if `changed_speed_recovery` = 1b and an EIOS or Electrical Idle is detected/inferred on any configured Lane and no configured Lane received a TS2 since entering this substate. New data rate after Recovery.Speed reverts to the speed at which Recovery was entered from L0 or L1.\n- **Recovery.Speed** — if `changed_speed_recovery` = 0b and current speed > 2.5 GT/s and EIOS or Electrical Idle detected/inferred on any configured Lane and no TS2 received since entering this substate. New data rate after Recovery.Speed: 2.5 GT/s (8b/10b or 128b/130b) or 32.0 GT/s (1b/1b).\n\nRegister Updates:\n- Data rate(s) advertised in received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets with `speed_change` set are noted as supported by the other Port.\n- **Autonomous Change** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) in received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets is noted by the Downstream Port for possible logging in the Link Status Register in Recovery.Speed.\n- **Upstream Ports** must register the **Selectable De-emphasis** bit (Symbol 4 bit 6) in received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets in `select_deemphasis`.\n- `changed_speed_recovery` and `directed_speed_change` are reset to 0b if LTSSM transitions to Configuration.\n- `changed_speed_recovery` and `directed_speed_change` are reset to 0b on entry to Recovery.Idle.\n- If the `N_FTS` value changed, the new value must be used for future L0s states.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- **Recovery.Idle** — if all of the following are true:\n  - Eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets received on all configured Lanes with matching Link and Lane numbers, `speed_change` = 0b, current data rate = 2.5 GT/s, and no 5.0 GT/s (or higher) data rate identifiers in received or transmitted TS2 Ordered Sets.\n  - 16 TS2 Ordered Sets sent after receiving one TS2 Ordered Set (without intervening EIEOS).\n- In Flit Mode: if received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets have `Hot_Reset_Request`, `Disable_Link_Request`, or `Loopback_Request` asserted on any configured Lane, the corresponding directed bit must be set.\n- **Configuration** (Non-Flit or Flit Mode) — per conditions above.\n- After a **48 ms** timeout:\n  - **Detect** — if current data rate is 2.5 GT/s or 5.0 GT/s.\n  - **Recovery.Idle** — if `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` < FFh and current data rate is 8.0 GT/s or higher.\n  - **Detect** — otherwise.\n\nNotes:\n- **IMPLEMENTATION NOTE: ISSUES WITH SOFTWARE INITIATED SPEED CHANGE.** In prior versions of the specification, EQ TS2 Ordered Sets from the Upstream Port may not be registered by the Downstream Port in the case of hardware-autonomous equalization not being adopted. If the Upstream Port switches from TS2 to EQ TS2 while in Recovery.RcvrCfg based on observing the new speed, the Downstream Port may have already satisfied its requirements with 8 consecutive standard TS2 Ordered Sets and missed the preset or precoding request in the subsequent EQ TS2 Ordered Sets. This can be avoided if the Upstream Port sends EQ TS2 Ordered Sets based on the higher speed being advertised by the Downstream Port while it is in Recovery.RcvrLock, or if software requests re-equalization at the same data rate via configuration registers in the Downstream Port.\n- **IMPLEMENTATION NOTE:** The above requirement ensures that both components immediately exit Recovery.Idle to the corresponding Hot Reset/Disable/Loopback state without the follower having to send a data stream and wait for the next SKP Ordered Set boundary.\n- **IMPLEMENTATION NOTE: REDUCING LINK WIDTH DUE TO ERRORS IN FLIT MODE.** With Flit Mode, since there is no upconfig support, the reason for a Recovery → Configuration transition is to reduce the Link width due to errors. The Port wanting to reduce Link width should send PAD in the Link Number field on Lanes it does not want to be part of the configured Link.\n" }
  },
  "Recovery.Idle": {
    specChapter: "4.2.7.4.5",
    sourceFile: "recovery.idle.md",
    markdown: { value: "Chapter: 4.2.7.4.5\n\nDescription:\n- Transmitter sends Idle data Symbols on all configured Lanes in Non-Flit Mode (8b/10b), or IDLE Flits across all configured Lanes in Flit Mode.\n- When using 128b/130b encoding in Non-Flit Mode:\n  - At 8.0 GT/s: Transmitter sends one SDS Ordered Set on all configured Lanes to start a Data Stream and then sends Idle data Symbols. The first Idle data Symbol on Lane 0 is the first Symbol of the Data Stream.\n  - At 16.0 GT/s or higher: Transmitter sends one Control SKP Ordered Set followed immediately by one SDS Ordered Set on all configured Lanes, then sends Idle data Symbols.\n- When using 1b/1b encoding or 128b/130b encoding in Flit Mode: Transmitter sends one SDS Ordered Set sequence followed by a Control SKP Ordered Set on all configured Lanes followed by IDLE Flits to start a Data Stream.\n\nEntry Actions:\n- N/A\n\nTx Behavior:\n- If directed to other states (**Disabled**, **Hot Reset**, **Configuration**, or **Loopback**): Idle Symbols / IDLE Flits do not have to be sent (and must not be sent with 128b/130b encoding) before transitioning.\n\nRx Expectations:\n- **Disabled** — if directed (Downstream/optional crosslink Port instructed by higher layer to assert `Disable_Link_Request`, or Upstream Port in Flit Mode with `Disable_Link_Request` in Training Control of the eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets that triggered this state).\n- **Hot Reset** — if directed (Downstream/optional crosslink Port instructed by higher layer to assert `Hot_Reset_Request`, or Upstream/optional crosslink Port in Flit Mode with `Hot_Reset_Request` in Training Control of received TS2 Ordered Sets).\n- **Configuration** — if directed (Port instructed by higher layer to optionally re-configure the Link, e.g., different width Link).\n- **Loopback** — if directed and Transmitter is capable of being a Loopback Lead (implementation-specific); Port becomes the Loopback Lead.\n- **Disabled** (Non-Flit Mode, Upstream/optional crosslink Ports only) — immediately after any configured Lane has `Disable_Link_Request` asserted in two consecutive received TS1 Ordered Sets.\n- **Hot Reset** (Non-Flit Mode, Upstream/optional crosslink Ports only) — immediately after any configured Lane has `Hot_Reset_Request` asserted in two consecutive received TS1 Ordered Sets.\n- **Configuration** — if two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets are received on any configured Lane with Lane number set to PAD. (A Port optionally transitioning to change Link configuration is guaranteed to send Lane numbers set to PAD on all Lanes.)\n- **Loopback** — if any configured Lane has `Loopback_Request` asserted in two consecutive TS1 Ordered Sets (Non-Flit Mode), or if the received eight consecutive TS2 Ordered Sets that resulted in the transition to this state had `Loopback_Request` asserted in the Training Control field in any configured Lane (Flit Mode). The receiving device becomes the Loopback Follower.\n- **L0** (8b/10b Non-Flit Mode) — eight consecutive Symbol Times of Idle data received on all configured Lanes and 16 Idle data Symbols sent after receiving one Idle data Symbol.\n- **L0** (128b/130b Non-Flit Mode) — eight consecutive Symbol Times of Idle data received on all configured Lanes and 16 Idle data Symbols sent after receiving one Idle Symbol, and this state was not entered by a timeout from Recovery.RcvrCfg. Idle data Symbols must be received in Data Blocks. Lane-to-Lane de-skew must be completed before Data Stream processing starts.\n- **L0** (Flit Mode) — two consecutive IDLE Flits received and the minimum number of IDLE Flits sent after receiving one IDLE Flit, provided this state was not entered by a timeout from Recovery.RcvrCfg. Minimum IDLE Flits to send: 4 (8b/10b or 128b/130b encoding) or 8 (1b/1b encoding). Lane-to-Lane de-skew must be completed before Data Stream processing starts.\n\nRegister Updates:\n- If software has written 1b to the **Retrain Link** bit in the Link Control Register since the last transition to L0 from Recovery or Configuration, the Downstream Port must set the **Link Bandwidth Management Status** bit of the Link Status Register to 1b.\n- `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` is reset to 00h on transition to L0.\n\nTiming / Exit Conditions:\n- After a **2 ms** timeout:\n  - **Recovery.RcvrLock** — if `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` < FFh.\n    - At 8.0 GT/s or higher: `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` is incremented by 1 upon transitioning to Recovery.RcvrLock.\n    - At 5.0 GT/s (or 2.5 GT/s if supported): `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` is set to FFh upon transitioning to Recovery.RcvrLock.\n  - **Detect** — if `idle_to_rlock_transitioned` = FFh.\n\nNotes:\n- In 128b/130b encoding in Non-Flit Mode, on transition to Configuration, Loopback, Hot Reset, or Disabled, an EDS must be sent if a Data Stream is active (i.e., an SDS Ordered Set has been sent). The side not initiating Link Upconfigure may have already transmitted SDS and be transmitting a Data Stream (Logical IDL); in that case it will send EDS on active Lanes before sending TS1 Ordered Sets in Configuration.\n- It is recommended that in Non-Flit Mode the LTSSM initiate Link width up/downsizing using the PAD-Lane-number transition to reduce the time to change the Link width. In Flit Mode, the Recovery.RcvrCfg → Configuration transition should be used instead.\n" }
  }
};
